\begin{quote}
Sol: The correct answer is: (b) To train officials on the revised fund
flow procedure under MPLAD Scheme.

Explanation:\\
• The e-SAKSHI Portal workshop was aimed at providing hands-on training
to officials regarding the revised fund flow procedure under the MPLAD
Scheme (Members of Parliament Local Area Development Scheme).

• The training focuses on ensuring the efficient and transparent
management of funds allocated under the MPLAD scheme, which allows
Members of Parliament (MPs) to recommend developmental projects in their
constituencies.

Information Booster:\\
• The MPLAD Scheme enables MPs to contribute towards the local
development of their constituencies by recommending specific projects
for funding.

• The e-SAKSHI Portal is part of the initiative to ensure better
accountability and tracking of these projects, improving the fund
disbursement process.

Q.3 \textbf{Which of the following statements based on the Union List
entries is/are correct?}

\textbf{1. The Parliament has exclusive power to legislate on lotteries
conducted by both the Central and State governments.}

\textbf{2.Matters related to the incorporation and regulation of
cooperative societies are included in the Union List.}

\textbf{3.The Reserve Bank of India, foreign exchange, and currency are
all under the legislative domain of the Union.}\\
\textbf{4.Parliament can make laws regarding corporations whose
activities are not limited to one State, excluding universities.}

A. Only 1, 3, and 4\\
B. Only 2 and 3\\
C. Only 1 and 4\\
D. All four statements 1,2,3 and 4 are correct
\end{quote}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 2\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 2\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5000}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) Only 1, 3, and 4.

Explanation:\\
• Statement 1 is correct: The Parliament has the exclusive power to
legislate on lotteries conducted by both the central and state
governments.• Statement 2 is incorrect: Cooperative societies are
included in the Concurrent List, not the Union List.

• Statement 3 is correct: The Reserve Bank of India, foreign exchange,
and currency are all under the jurisdiction of the Union government.
\end{quote}\strut
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=0.58333in,height=1.02778in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image1.png}
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
• Statement 4 is correct: The Parliament has the authority to legislate
on corporations whose activities are not limited to one state.

Schedules and Articles Related to Federalism in India:\\
• Article 1: Defines India as a Union of States and ensures the federal
structure.

• Article 246: Details the distribution of powers between the Union and
States, which is categorized into Union List, State List, and Concurrent
List under the Seventh Schedule.

• Seventh Schedule:\\
· Union List: Matters on which only the Central Government can
legislate, e.g., defense, foreign affairs, and banking.

· State List: Matters on which only the State Government can legislate,
e.g., police, public health, and education.

· Concurrent List: Matters on which both the Union and State governments
can legislate, e.g., criminal law, marriage, and bankruptcy.

Q.4 \textbf{Which ISRO centers participate in the International
Geosphere-Biosphere Programme?}

A. ISRO Headquarters, Space Applications Centre, National Remote Sensing
Centre, Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre\\
B. Space Physics Laboratory, National Remote Sensing Centre, Indian
Institute of Remote Sensing, Physical Research Laboratory\\
C. Physical Research Laboratory, Space Applications Centre, Indian
Institute of Remote Sensing, ISRO Satellite Centre\\
D. Space Application Centre (SAC), Indian Institute of Remote Sensing,
Space Applications Centre, National Remote Sensing Centre

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Space Application Centre (SAC), Indian
Institute of Remote Sensing, Space Applications Centre, National Remote
Sensing Centre.

Explanation:\\
• The Space Applications Centre (SAC), Indian Institute of Remote
Sensing (IIRS), and National Remote Sensing Centre (NRSC) are key ISRO
centers that contribute to earth observation, climate research, and
environmental monitoring, which are integral to the International
Geosphere-Biosphere Programme (IGBP).
\end{quote}

• These centers are involved in remote sensing and geospatial data
collection that aids in global research on climate change, environmental
conservation, and the study of earth systems.

\begin{quote}
History and Facts about ISRO:\\
• ISRO (Indian Space Research Organisation), founded in 1969 by Dr.
Vikram Sarabhai, is the space agency of India, and has become one of the
world's leading space organizations.• ISRO's First Satellite Launch:
India's first satellite, Aryabhata, was launched in 1975 with the help
of the Soviet Union.

• ISRO has developed significant capabilities in satellite
communication, weather forecasting, remote sensing, and space
exploration.

• The Chandrayaan-1 mission in 2008 was India's first mission to the
moon and successfully confirmed the presence of water molecules on the
lunar surface.

• Mangalyaan: ISRO\textquotesingle s Mars Orbiter Mission (MOM),
launched in 2013, made India the first Asian country to reach Mars orbit
and the fourth space agency in the world to do so.

• ISRO's PSLV (Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle) has become world-renowned
for its efficiency and low-cost missions, allowing the agency to launch
satellites for both India and other countries.

Key Contributions and Projects:\\
· ISRO has launched over 300 satellites in multiple missions,
contributing to global space research.

· The IRNSS (Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System) provides India
with regional GPS-like capabilities and serves various applications like
navigation, mapping, and disaster management.

· ISRO's Gaganyaan Mission aims to send Indian astronauts into space,
further advancing India's space exploration capabilities.
\end{quote}

