Q.5 \textbf{Which government body was responsible for implementing the
Technology Mission on Oilseeds?}

A. Ministry of Defense\\
B. Ministry of Commerce\\
C. Ministry of Education\\
D. Ministry of Agriculture

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Ministry of Agriculture.

Explanation:\\
• The Technology Mission on Oilseeds was launched by the Ministry of
Agriculture in 1986-87 to improve oilseed production in India.\\
• The mission aimed to increase domestic production of edible oils,
reduce dependency on imports, and enhance the livelihoods of farmers.\\
• It focused on improving technology in the production and processing of
oilseeds like groundnut, soybean, and rapeseed.

Information Booster:\\
• The mission was successful in enhancing the productivity of oilseeds,
making India one of the largest oilseed producers globally.

• The program was later integrated into the broader National Mission on
Oilseeds and Oil Palm to further boost domestic oil production.

Q.6 \textbf{Read the statements given below:}\\
\textbf{Statement 1: The Constitution of India divides the legislative
subjects into 3 lists, namely the Centre, State, and Concurrent List.}

\textbf{Statement 2: The subject of \textquotesingle Protection of wild
animals and birds\textquotesingle{} is transferred to the Concurrent
list from the State list by the 42nd Amendment Act. Statement 3: Only
the Parliament of India can make laws on the subjects under the
Concurrent List.}

\textbf{Which of the following statements given above is/are correct?}

A. Only Statements 1 and 3 are correct\\
B. All three statements 1, 2, and 3 are correct\\
C. Only Statement 2 is correct\\
D. Only Statements 1 and 2 are correct

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Only Statements 1 and 2 are correct.

Explanation:\\
• Statement 1 is correct: The Constitution of India divides legislative
subjects into three lists: the Union List, State List, and Concurrent
List (Article 246).

• Statement 2 is correct: The subject of \textquotesingle Protection of
wild animals and birds\textquotesingle{} was moved to the Concurrent
List from the State List by the 42nd Amendment Act of 1976.

• Statement 3 is incorrect: Both the Parliament and State Legislatures
can make laws on subjects in the Concurrent List. If there is a
conflict, the law made by Parliament prevails (Article 254).

Information Booster:\\
• The Union List contains subjects on which only the Central Government
can legislate, such as defense, foreign affairs, and banking.

• The State List contains subjects on which only the State Governments
can legislate, such as police, public health, and education.

• The Concurrent List contains subjects on which both the Union and
State governments can legislate, such as criminal law, marriage, and
bankruptcy.

Related Articles and Amendments:\\
• Article 246: Deals with the distribution of legislative powers between
the Union and the States.

• Article 254: Provides that in case of a conflict between Union and
State laws in the Concurrent List, the Union law will prevail.

• 42nd Amendment Act (1976): This amendment was significant for shifting
the \textquotesingle Protection of wild animals and
birds\textquotesingle{} to the Concurrent List, enhancing the central
government\textquotesingle s role in wildlife protection.

Q.7 \textbf{Given below are two statements marked as Assertion (A) and
Reason (R). Read and mark the correct options:}\\
\textbf{Assertion (A): The Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023,
promotes e-filing and virtual trials to expedite the justice delivery
system. Reason (R): The integration of technology aims to reduce case
pendency and enhance accessibility to justice.}

A. Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\\
B. A is false, but R is true.

C. Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\\
D. A is true, but R is false.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) Both A and R are true, and R is the
correct explanation of A.

Explanation:\\
• Assertion (A) is true: The Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNS),
2023, aims to reform India's criminal justice system by introducing
e-filing, virtual trials, and other technological innovations. This is
aimed at reducing the delays in the justice delivery system.

• Reason (R) is true: By incorporating technology, the BNS seeks to
reduce the pendency of cases and ensure more efficient and accessible
justice, particularly benefiting marginalized sections of society.

• The integration of technology enables remote hearings and virtual
trials, making the judicial process more transparent and inclusive.

Additional Facts about BNS:\\
• Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita is a significant step towards
modernizing the judicial system.

• The e-filing system and virtual trials were introduced to help reduce
backlogs of cases, a persistent issue in India's overburdened judicial
system.

• The BNS aims to bring equity in justice by ensuring that people have
better access to the courts through digital platforms.

Q.8 \textbf{Consider the following statements regarding the Ghaghara
River system:}\\
\textbf{1. The Ghaghara originates from the Mapchachungo glacier in the
Himalayas.}\\
\textbf{2. It forms a deep gorge at Shishapani before entering the
plains.}

\textbf{3. It merges with the Ganga at Allahabad after receiving the
Sarda River.}\\
\textbf{Which of the above statements is/are correct?}

A. Only 1 and 3\\
B. Only 1 and 2\\
C. Only 2 and 3\\
D. 1, 2, and 3 are correct

Answer: B

Sol: The correct answer is: (b) Only 1 and 2.

Explanation:\\
• Statement 1 is correct: The Ghaghara River originates from the
Mapchachungo Glacier in the Tibetan Plateau, in the Himalayas, and flows
through Nepal and northern India.• Statement 2 is correct: The river
forms a deep gorge at Shishapani, which is a significant geological
feature in the region.
\end{quote}

• Statement 3 is incorrect: The Ghaghara does not merge with the Ganga
at Allahabad. Instead, it merges with the Ganges River near Chhapra in
Bihar, after receiving the Sarda River.

\begin{quote}
Additional Information about Rivers:\\
· Ghaghara River:\\
· Originates from the Mapchachungo Glacier in the Himalayas, flows
through Nepal, and enters India through the Kushinagar region.

· It is a tributary of the Ganga and contributes significantly to the
river system in Bihar.

· Ganga River:\\
· Originates from the Gangotri Glacier and flows through northern India
into Bangladesh.

· It is one of the longest rivers in the world, serving as a lifeline
for millions of people in India.

· Sarda River:\\
· Originates in the Uttarakhand hills and joins the Ghaghara in Bihar,
contributing to the water flow of the Ghaghara River.
\end{quote}

