Explanation: Copyright © 2026 Adda247

• Cellulose is the primary structural component of the plant cell wall.
It provides rigidity and helps plants maintain their shape and resist
turgor pressure.
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

ostly of cellulose (a carbohydrate polymer), not proteins. This gives
the plant its strength and ability to grow upright.

Information Booster:\\
• Other Important Facts about Cells:\\
· Plasmolysis (Option a): Refers to the process where a plant cell loses
water and the cell membrane pulls away from the cell wall due to osmotic
pressure. It occurs when the cell is placed in a hypertonic solution
(higher solute concentration outside the cell).

· Cell Wall (Option b): The cell wall is primarily composed of
cellulose, not protein. While proteins like collagen are important in
animal cells, plants rely on cellulose for structure.

· Plasma Membrane (Option d): In plant cells, the plasma membrane is
located inside the cell wall, not outside. The plasma membrane is a
selectively permeable membrane that regulates the entry and exit of
materials in and out of the cell.

Additional Knowledge:\\
• Eukaryotic vs. Prokaryotic Cells: In eukaryotic cells, the presence of
membrane-bound organelles like the nucleus and mitochondria sets them
apart from prokaryotic cells (e.g., bacteria), which lack such
organelles.

• The mitochondria in eukaryotic cells are often referred to as the
"powerhouses" of the cell, as they produce ATP through cellular
respiration.

• The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) comes in two types: rough ER (with
ribosomes, involved in protein synthesis) and smooth ER (involved in
lipid synthesis).

Q.13 \textbf{Which of the following plateaus is located in the
northeastern part of India?}

A. Malwa Plateau\\
B. Deccan Plateau\\
C. Chota Nagpur Plateau\\
D. Shillong Plateau

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Shillong Plateau.

Explanation:\\
• The Shillong Plateau is located in the northeastern part of India,
primarily in Meghalaya. It is part of the Eastern Himalayas and is known
for its scenic beauty, hills, and rich biodiversity.• The Shillong
Plateau is an elevated region and serves as a geological formation
separating the Brahmaputra Valley and Bay of Bengal drainage systems.

Information Booster:\\
• Chota Nagpur Plateau (Option c): This plateau is located in the
eastern part of India, mainly in Jharkhand, Bihar, and Odisha, and is
known for its mineral resources.

• Deccan Plateau (Option b): Located in the southern part of India,
covering areas like Maharashtra, Karnataka, and Andhra Pradesh.

• Malwa Plateau (Option a): Located in the central region of India,
primarily in Madhya Pradesh.

Q.14 \textbf{The cell uses internal compartmentalization to manage its
complexity. Which of the following best reflects this idea?}

A. Organelles in eukaryotic cells float freely without any specific
role.

B. Prokaryotic cells use multiple membranes to isolate cell activities.

C. Eukaryotic cells have membrane-bound organelles that separate
different functions.\\
D. All cellular functions occur in the same space without any
separation.

Answer: C

Sol: The correct answer is: (c) Eukaryotic cells have membrane-bound
organelles that separate different functions.

Explanation:\\
• Eukaryotic cells have internal compartmentalization through
membrane-bound organelles such as the nucleus, mitochondria, and
endoplasmic reticulum, which separate different cellular functions.

• This allows for more efficient management of the
cell\textquotesingle s complexity and ensures that specialized tasks can
be carried out in different areas of the cell without interference.

Information Booster:\\
• Prokaryotic Cells (Option b) lack membrane-bound organelles, and their
internal functions take place in the cytoplasm.

• Membrane-bound organelles in eukaryotic cells allow for specialization
and organization, critical for processes like DNA replication, energy
production, and protein synthesis.

Additional Knowledge:\\
• Nucleus: In eukaryotic cells, the nucleus houses the genetic material
(DNA) and is essential for regulating cell functions like growth,
division, and differentiation.

• Mitochondria: Known as the powerhouse of the cell, mitochondria
produce ATP, which provides energy for the cell's activities.

• Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER): The rough ER is involved in protein
synthesis, while the smooth ER is involved in lipid synthesis and
detoxification processes.

Q.15 \textbf{Which AQI range corresponds to "Unhealthy for Sensitive
Groups"?}

A. 101-150\\
B. 151-203\\
C. 0-50\\
D. 51-100

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) 101-150.

Explanation:\\
• The Air Quality Index (AQI) is a scale used to measure and report air
quality.

• The AQI range 101-150 is categorized as ``Unhealthy for Sensitive
Groups'', which means that people with pre-existing respiratory or heart
conditions, children, and the elderly may experience adverse health
effects, while the general population may not be as affected.

• This range suggests that air quality is moderately polluted, and
individuals with respiratory issues should avoid prolonged exposure.

Information Booster:\\
• AQI is categorized into several ranges:\\
· 0-50: Good - Air quality is considered satisfactory, and air pollution
poses little or no risk.

· 51-100: Moderate - Air quality is acceptable; however, some pollutants
may be a concern for a few sensitive individuals.

· 151-200: Unhealthy - Everyone may begin to experience health effects,
and sensitive groups may experience more severe health effects.

· 201-300: Very Unhealthy - Health warnings of emergency conditions.

· 301-500: Hazardous - Serious health effects for everyone.

Q.16 \textbf{Which of the following is considered the oldest known
civilization of the Indian subcontinent?}

A. Gupta Empire\\
B. Maurya Civilization\\
C. Indus Valley Civilization\\
D. Vedic Civilization

Answer: C

Sol: The correct answer is: (c) Indus Valley Civilization.

Explanation:\\
• The Indus Valley Civilization, also known as the Harappan
Civilization, is the oldest known civilization of the Indian
subcontinent.

• It flourished around 2500 BCE to 1900 BCE in what is now modern-day
Pakistan and parts of India, particularly in the Indus River basin.

• The civilization is known for its advanced urban planning, drainage
systems, and well-organized cities like Harappa and Mohenjo-Daro.

Information Booster:\\
• Gupta Empire (a): Known for its cultural achievements, the Gupta
Empire flourished from the 4th to the 6th century CE, long after the
Indus Valley Civilization.• Maurya Civilization (b): The Maurya Empire,
founded by Chandragupta Maurya in c. 322 BCE, was significant in Indian
history but came after the Indus Valley.• Vedic Civilization (d): The
Vedic Civilization emerged after the Indus Valley Civilization, around
1500 BCE.
\end{quote}

