Central Asia, bypassing Pakistan.

Explanation:\\
• Chabahar Port, located in Iran, is strategically significant for
India, as it provides direct access to Afghanistan and Central Asia,
allowing India to bypass Pakistan's land routes.• India has invested in
developing Chabahar Port to enhance trade routes, secure energy
supplies, and establish its presence in the Persian Gulf region.

• The port also offers India a more secure alternative for transporting
goods to landlocked Afghanistan and other central Asian countries,
fostering stronger economic and strategic ties.

Information Booster:\\
• Chabahar Port is considered part of India's ``Look East'' policy,
aimed at increasing connectivity with the Middle East, Central Asia, and
Afghanistan.

• India is developing the port with the help of Iran, with plans for
trade corridors, infrastructure development, and investment in the
region. The development of the port is seen as a counterbalance to
China's influence in the region, especially in the Gwadar Port in
Pakistan.

Q.21 \textbf{In which state is the folk music tradition of "Zikir"
popular?}

A. Assam\\
B. Gujarat\\
C. Odisha\\
D. Rajasthan

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) Assam.

Explanation:\\
• The Zikir is a traditional folk music form that is particularly
popular in the state of Assam.

• It is often associated with the Bhakti movement and is performed in
Sattras (Vaishnavite monasteries) and during religious and cultural
gatherings.

• The Zikir tradition is deeply rooted in Assamese culture and often
involves devotional songs that are sung in praise of Lord Krishna and
other deities.

Information Booster:\\
• Assam: The Zikir is part of the Neo-Vaishnavism tradition, founded by
Srimanta Sankardev, and is performed during Borgeet (devotional songs),
Nam Kirtan, and other religious ceremonies.• Gujarat (Option b): Known
for its Garba and Dandiya dances, but Zikir is not a part of Gujarat's
traditional music.

• Odisha (Option c): While Odisha has its own rich tradition of music,
including Odissi and Gotipua, it is not known for Zikir.

• Rajasthan (Option d): Famous for its Ghoomar, Kalbeliya, and Marwari
music, but not Zikir.

Additional Knowledge:\\
• Zikir is often accompanied by Khol (a type of drum), Taal, and other
traditional instruments, creating a rhythmic and melodic atmosphere
during performances.

Q.22 \textbf{The concept of "opportunity cost" in economics refers to:}

A. The value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is
made.\\
B. The cost incurred in the past that cannot be recovered.\\
C. The cost of all alternatives that are not chosen.

D. The monetary cost of the most expensive alternative.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) The value of the next best alternative
forgone when a choice is made.

Explanation:\\
• Opportunity cost refers to the value of the next best alternative that
must be given up when a choice is made.

• It is a fundamental concept in economics that helps explain how
resources (time, money, effort) are allocated to maximize benefits.\\
• By choosing one option, the opportunity cost is the benefit that could
have been gained from the next best alternative.

Information Booster:\\
• Opportunity cost is relevant in all decision-making processes, such as
whether to invest in a project or spend time on a different activity.

• It's not just the monetary cost but also includes time, effort, or
enjoyment lost by not choosing the next best alternative.

Q.23 \textbf{What was the recommendation regarding entry of foreign
banks in India?}

A. Allow foreign banks to operate freely\\
B. Restrict foreign banks to urban areas\\
C. Ban foreign banks in India\\
D. Encourage foreign banks to expand operations in India

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Encourage foreign banks to expand
operations in India.

Explanation:\\
• The entry of foreign banks into India was encouraged to foster greater
competition, improve banking services, and bring in international
banking expertise.

• The government, along with the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), has
facilitated the operations of foreign banks in India, allowing them to
enhance the banking environment in the country.

Information Booster:\\
• RBI Regulations: The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has set regulations
for foreign banks to ensure that they comply with Indian banking laws,
such as maintaining capital adequacy ratios, following anti-money
laundering norms, and ensuring fair lending practices.

• Entry of Foreign Banks: Foreign banks are allowed to operate in India
either by opening branches or setting up wholly owned subsidiaries. In
some cases, they are allowed to acquire domestic banks, further
integrating global financial systems with India\textquotesingle s
economy.

Additional Knowledge:\\
• History of RBI and Banking in India:\\
· The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) was established in 1935 as a part of
the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934. It began as a private bank but was
nationalized in 1949 to align with India's economic policies.

· Indian Banking Sector Development: After Independence, India saw the
nationalization of several major banks, starting with 14 major
commercial banks in 1969 under then-Prime Minister Indira Gandhi. This
aimed to increase the government's control over the banking sector and
promote financial inclusion.

· Liberalization: The 1991 economic reforms brought liberalization to
India, allowing more private and foreign banks to enter the market,
leading to increased competition and modernized banking infrastructure.

· Foreign Bank Presence: Foreign banks like HSBC, Citibank, and Standard
Chartered are major players in India today, contributing significantly
to sectors such as investment banking, corporate banking, and retail
banking.

Q.24 \textbf{Which temple, built by the Chandela dynasty in the
10th-11th centuries, is known for its exquisite sculptures?}

A. Kandariya Mahadeva Temple\\
B. Brihadeeswarar Temple\\
C. Sun Temple, Modhera\\
D. Lingaraja Temple

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) Kandariya Mahadeva Temple.

Explanation:\\
• The Kandariya Mahadeva Temple, located in Khajuraho (Madhya Pradesh),
was built by the Chandela dynasty in the 10th-11th centuries.

• It is famous for its exquisite sculptures that adorn the temple's
walls, depicting various mythological scenes, deities, and celestial
beings.

• The temple is dedicated to Lord Shiva and is considered a masterpiece
of Indian architecture and art.

• It is part of the Khajuraho Group of Monuments, which is a UNESCO
World Heritage Site and is renowned for its stunning northern Indian
temple architecture.
\end{quote}

