high-performing districts and the backward districts, fostering
sustainable development across the country.• Districts are selected
based on their performance in various indicators such as health,
education, gender equality, and economic development.

Q.46 \textbf{Which major reform was introduced in the National Education
Policy (NEP), 2020?}

A. Replacement of the 10+2 system with a 5+3+3+4 structure\\
B. Elimination of private schools\\
C. Compulsory Sanskrit education\\
D. Mandatory board exams for all grades

Answer: A

Sol: The correct answer is: (a) Replacement of the 10+2 system with a
5+3+3+4 structure.

Explanation:\\
• One of the major reforms in the National Education Policy (NEP), 2020,
is the replacement of the traditional 10+2 system with a 5+3+3+4
structure for school education.• This new structure focuses on early
childhood care and education (ECCE) and a holistic approach to learning.

· 5 years: Foundational stage (ages 3--8)\\
· 3 years: Preparatory stage (ages 8--11)\\
· 3 years: Middle stage (ages 11--14)\\
· 4 years: Secondary stage (ages 14--18)\\
· • This reform aims to make education more inclusive, flexible, and
skills-oriented.

Information Booster:\\
• The NEP 2020 also promotes the teaching of multiple languages,
vocational education, and the use of technology in education.

• Compulsory Sanskrit education (Option c) is not a reform under the
NEP.

• The NEP does not mandate mandatory board exams for all grades (Option
d), but it seeks to reduce the stress of exams by introducing a more
flexible system.

Q.47 \textbf{Which of the following phenomena occurs when a wave passes
through a narrow slit?}

A. Refraction\\
B. Reflection\\
C. Absorption\\
D. Diffraction

Answer: D

Sol: The correct answer is: (d) Diffraction.

Explanation:\\
• Diffraction occurs when a wave (such as light, sound, or water waves)
passes through a narrow slit or around an obstacle.

• The wave spreads out after passing through the slit, and the amount of
diffraction increases as the size of the slit decreases relative to the
wavelength of the wave.• This is a key principle in wave theory and can
be observed in various scenarios, such as the bending of light through a
narrow aperture.

Information Booster:\\
• Refraction (Option a): Refraction is the bending of a wave as it
passes from one medium to another, such as light moving from air to
water.

• Reflection (Option b): Reflection occurs when a wave bounces back from
a surface, like light reflecting off a mirror.

• Absorption (Option c): Absorption occurs when a wave is absorbed by a
material, converting the wave's energy into another form, such as heat.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=0.26389in,height=0.22222in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image13.png}

\begin{quote}
Q.48 \textbf{What is the primary purpose of the Insolvency and
Bankruptcy Code (IBC), 2016?}

A. To regulate stock market investments\\
B. To promote foreign direct investment\\
C. To simplify the process of resolving financial distress in
businesses\\
D. To provide protection for startups from financial harassment

Answer: C

Sol: The correct answer is: (c) To simplify the process of resolving
financial distress in businesses.

Explanation:\\
• The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC), 2016, was enacted to
establish a streamlined and time-bound process for dealing with
corporate insolvency and bankruptcy in India.• The primary purpose of
the IBC is to provide a mechanism for the resolution of stressed assets
and to help businesses recover from financial distress, while also
ensuring that creditors get their due.

• The IBC aims to reduce the delay in bankruptcy proceedings and improve
the ease of doing business in India.

Information Booster:\\
• The IBC has been a game-changer for businesses in India, offering a
structured process for dealing with non-performing assets (NPAs) and
distressed companies.

• It also provides legal protection for businesses undergoing resolution
and has led to the establishment of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Board
of India (IBBI) to regulate the processes.

Q.49 \textbf{Given below are the Assertion and Reason, choose the
correct options regarding both the statements.}\\
\textbf{Assertion (A): Seasonal agricultural practices, such as stubble
burning, have no significant impact on AQI levels in India.}

\textbf{Reason (R): Stubble burning is a controlled practice that
doesn\textquotesingle t release substantial pollutants into the
atmosphere.}

A. A is true, but R is false.

B. Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.

C. Both Statement are Incorrect\\
D. Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.

Answer: C

Sol: The correct answer is: (C) A is false, but R is true\\
Explanation:

\includegraphics{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image14.png}
Assertion (A) is false because seasonal agricultural practices like
stubble burning significantly increase air pollution in India,
especially in northern states like Punjab and Haryana during
post-harvest periods. These practices sharply worsen the Air Quality
Index (AQI), leading to smog and health hazards.

\includegraphics{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image15.png}
Reason (R) the sense that stubble burning is a controlled agricultural
practice, but the statement ``doesn't release substantial pollutants''
is incorrect in context, because even controlled burning releases large
amounts of particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), CO, NOx, and greenhouse
gases.

