The passage argues that robust public transport networks (buses, trams,
metros) are ``pillars of urban sustainability,'' reduce congestion and
emissions, work best when fares are integrated, and need investment,
planning, and equity-focused coverage. This directly frames strong,
well-planned public transport as essential for cities that are both
sustainable (environmentally and economically) and inclusive (serving
low-income communities as well as commercial hubs).

\textbf{Explanation of context of passage}\\
The author outlines benefits (less reliance on private cars, fewer
traffic snarls, lower emissions), enablers (integrated fares, exclusive
lanes, priority signaling), requirements (capital and long-term vision),
and equity aims (serving low-income areas). The concluding line ties
these to economic efficiency, social mobility, and cleaner
cities---hallmarks of sustainable, inclusive urbanism.

\textbf{Other options are incorrect because:}\\
· (a) \emph{Public transport is outdated in modern cities}: The passage
presents public transport as modern and vital, not outdated.

· (b) \emph{Investment in roads for private vehicles is more practical}:
The author highlights the drawbacks of private-car reliance (snarls,
emissions) and advocates investment in transit priority, not roads for
cars.· (d) \emph{Public transport is mostly beneficial for tourists}:
The focus is on residents and equity across communities, not tourism.
