· (a) \emph{Create financial burden for low-income riders}: The passage
never claims integrated fares raise costs; it implies convenience, not
burden.

· (c) \emph{Are only for long-distance travel}: The example includes
local modes (bus, train, bike-share), not exclusively long-distance.

· (d) \emph{Encourage use of private vehicles}: The whole argument is
the opposite---integrated fares encourage public, not private,
transport.
