in asserting its dominance over the other, it paradoxically \_\_\_\_\_\_
(3) its own freedom.}

\textbf{The master, in reducing the slave to a mere instrument, deprives
himself of a fully realized recognition. Meanwhile, the slave, through
labour and self-transformation, attains a deeper awareness of autonomy,
demonstrating that freedom is not given but achieved through negation
and \_\_\_\_\_\_ (4). Spirit (Geist) emerges as the reconciliation of
these conflicts-not a static substance but a dynamic process of
self-realization. Spirit unfolds historically, manifesting itself (5) in
institutions, culture, and philosophy, gradually moving toward absolute
knowing. This historical progression is not linear but dialectical,
driven by the negation of old forms and the emergence of new ones.}

A. Yield\\
B. Justification\\
C. Detachment\\
D. Retraction

Answer: C

Sol: The word that can be filled in the blank is `Detachment' to make
the sentence grammatically and contextually correct.

• `Detachment' (noun) denotes ``a state of objective distance or
disinterestedness; disengagement from immediacy.'' In Hegelian-style
development, freedom is achieved through negation and a reflective
\textbf{detachment} from immediacy that enables self-overcoming.

• As per the context, the word `detachment' fits in very well \& hence,
it is the correct option.

\textbf{Meanings of other given words and why they are incorrect:}\\
• \textbf{Yield} -- as noun/verb (``produce/give way''); ``negation and
yield'' is semantically awkward in this philosophical frame.

• \textbf{Justification} -- ``reasoning that defends a belief/action'';
this is about giving reasons, not the experiential process of
liberation.

• \textbf{Retraction} -- ``withdrawal of a statement/claim''; too narrow
and specific; doesn't capture the dialectical self-transformation at
stake.

• Thus, all the other given words have different meanings and do not fit
the philosophical process being described.
\end{quote}

