\begin{quote}
\textbf{In the following question, a sentence with a blank is given,
followed by five words labeled (1), (2), (3), (4), and (5). Choose the
word that fills the blank appropriately in the context of the passage.}

\textbf{The truth of the self-conscious individual lies not in its
immediate certainty, but in the dialectical movement of its becoming.
Consciousness, in its pursuit of knowledge, initially assumes that its
object is something external and independent. Yet, through the labour of
experience, it discovers that this object is not truly separate but
\_\_\_\_\_\_(1) by consciousness itself.}

\textbf{The apparent duality of subject and object is thus sublated in
the dialectic, revealing that knowledge is not the passive reception of
an external truth but an active process in which consciousness shapes
and \_\_\_\_\_\_ (2) its own reality.}

\textbf{This movement unfolds through a series of contradictions, in
which each stage of consciousness, believing itself to have attained
certainty, encounters its own inadequacy. The master-slave dialectic
exemplifies this: self-consciousness seeks recognition from another, yet
in asserting its dominance over the other, it paradoxically \_\_\_\_\_\_
(3) its own freedom.}

\textbf{The master, in reducing the slave to a mere instrument, deprives
himself of a fully realized recognition. Meanwhile, the slave, through
labour and self-transformation, attains a deeper awareness of autonomy,
demonstrating that freedom is not given but achieved through negation
and \_\_\_\_\_\_ (4). Spirit (Geist) emerges as the reconciliation of
these conflicts-not a static substance but a dynamic process of
self-realization. Spirit unfolds historically, manifesting itself (5) in
institutions, culture, and philosophy, gradually moving toward absolute
knowing. This historical progression is not linear but dialectical,
driven by the negation of old forms and the emergence of new ones.}

A. Possessive pronoun\\
B. Demonstrative pronoun\\
C. Reflexive pronoun\\
D. Interrogative pronouns

Answer: C

Sol: The (5) item in the sentence is the pronoun `itself', which makes
the sentence grammatically and contextually correct.

• `Itself' is a \textbf{reflexive pronoun} used when the subject and the
object refer to the same entity (``Spirit \ldots{} manifesting
\textbf{itself} \ldots''). It marks that Spirit is both the agent and
the one in which the manifestation occurs.

• As per the context, identifying `itself' as a reflexive pronoun fits
in very well \& hence, it is the correct option.

\textbf{Meanings of other given options and why they are incorrect:}\\
• \textbf{Possessive pronoun} -- shows ownership (e.g., ``its,''
``mine,'' ``ours''), not reflexivity.

• \textbf{Demonstrative pronoun} -- points out specific things (e.g.,
``this,'' ``that,'' ``these,'' ``those'').

• \textbf{Interrogative pronouns} -- used to ask questions (e.g.,
``who,'' ``what,'' ``which,'' ``whom'').

• Thus, all the other options denote different pronoun types and do not
match `itself' in the sentence.
\end{quote}

