\begin{quote}
\textbf{Read the following passage and answer the questions based on the
passage:}\\
\textbf{Wildlife conservation has increased in importance since
governments and NGOs have realized that locals are vital guardians of
the environment. Instead of mandating top-down orders, successful
initiatives recruit villagers to protect threatened species, serve as
informants, and generate income from ecotourism. In other parts of
Africa and Asia, community rangers are taught tracking and first aid,
while women\textquotesingle s organizations produce handicrafts out of
sustainable forest products. These programs not only restrict poaching
but also create ownership and pride in the maintenance of biodiversity.
There are still obstacles, however: low funding levels, humanwildlife
conflict, and uncertain land-use rights can undo success. Continued
dialogue between stakeholders and equitable benefit-sharing mechanisms
are essential in guaranteeing that conservation outcomes are directed to
community well-being.}

A. Criticize eco-tourism for harming wildlife\\
B. Describe the role of communities in conservation\\
C. Explain how poachers operate\\
D. Advocate for stricter government enforcement

Answer: B

Sol: The correct answer is option (b).

The passage centers on how local communities are central to modern
wildlife conservation. It explains that governments/NGOs now partner
with villagers---training community rangers, engaging women's groups to
make sustainable handicrafts, and tapping ecotourism---so protection
efforts become locally owned and economically rewarding. It also notes
real-world hurdles (funding, human--wildlife conflict, land rights) and
stresses dialogue and benefit-sharing, all of which revolve around
community roles.

\textbf{Explanation of context of passage}\\
Overall, the paragraph contrasts older ``top-down'' approaches with
participatory, community-based conservation. It showcases practical ways
locals contribute and outlines conditions needed for success.

\textbf{Other options are incorrect because:}\\
· (a) The passage does not attack ecotourism; it cites ecotourism as a
positive income source that supports conservation, not something that
harms wildlife.

· (c) There is no description of poachers' methods; the focus is on
community participation, training, and benefits.

· (d) Rather than calling for stricter enforcement from above, it
emphasizes moving away from ``top-down orders'' toward community-led
strategies and equitable benefit-sharing.
\end{quote}

