\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

Answer: D

Sol: Option (d) contains an error.

\textbf{Detailed explanation:} After the expression \textbf{``It is
(high/about) time + subject''}, we normally use the \textbf{simple past}
(the mandative/subjunctive past) to express a \textbf{present}
recommendation/urgency about something overdue. Therefore, ``It is high
time \textbf{you started} studying seriously'' is correct---not ``you
start.'' (Hindi gist: ``अबबहुतहोचुका---तुम्हेंअबपढ़ाईशुरूकरदेनीचाहिए''
काभावव्यक्तकरनेकेलिए past form `started' प्रयुक्तहोताहै।)\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· Structure: \emph{It is (high/about) time + subject +
\textbf{V2}(simple past)} to express present urgency/overdue action.

· Compare: \emph{It's time to + V1} when the subject is implicit/same.

\textbf{Example:}\\
· Correct: \emph{It's high time we \textbf{left}for the station.}

· Incorrect: \emph{It's high time we \textbf{leave}for the station.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· You may also see the \textbf{subjunctive} base form after \emph{It's
time} in very formal or certain dialects, but standard exam usage
prefers the \textbf{simple past} (V2).

· With an infinitive: \emph{It's time \textbf{to}start.} / \emph{It's
time \textbf{for you to}start.}

Q.117 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence. The man is blind of one eye.}

A. with one eye\\
B. by one eye\\
C. in one eye\\
D. from one eye

Answer: C

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (c) \textbf{in
one eye}.

\textbf{Explanation:} In standard English, the adjective \textbf{blind}
takes the preposition \textbf{in} when referring to the affected organ:
\emph{blind \textbf{in}one eye / blind \textbf{in}the left eye}. ``Blind
of one eye'' is archaic/non-standard today.``With,'' ``by,'' and
``from'' do not idiomatically express the idea of impairment of a body
part in this construction.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:} With adjectives denoting physical
impairment (blind, deaf, lame, paralyzed), we use \textbf{in + body
part} to indicate the locus of impairment (e.g., \emph{deaf
\textbf{in}one ear}, \emph{lame \textbf{in}one leg}).
\textbf{Example:}\emph{She is \textbf{deaf in}one ear but hears
perfectly with the other.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· \textbf{Blind to} means ``unaware/insensible to'' ( \emph{blind to the
risks}), not physical blindness.

· Older usage like ``blind of one eye'' appears in historical texts but
is not preferred in modern standard usage.

Q.118 \textbf{Choose the most suitable option to replace the highlighted
part of the sentence. If the sentence is already correct, select
\textquotesingle No substitution required\textquotesingle.} \textbf{Each
of the candidates have submitted their documents for verification.}

A. had submitted their document\\
B. No substitution required\\
C. has submitted their documents\\
D. is submitted their documents

Answer: C

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (c)
\textbf{has submitted their documents}.

\textbf{Explanation:} With \textbf{each of + plural noun}, the
\textbf{grammatical subject is singular}, so the verb must be
\textbf{singular} ( \textbf{has}, not \textbf{have}). The pronoun
\textbf{their} is acceptable in modern standard English as a
\textbf{singular, gender-neutral} determiner/pronoun referring back to
\textbf{each} (instead of ``his or her''). ``Documents'' remains plural
because, in verification contexts, each candidate typically submits
multiple documents.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{Subject--Verb Agreement:}\emph{each / every / either /
neither} → \textbf{singular verb}.

· \textbf{Singular they:} Acceptable in modern usage: \emph{Each student
must bring \textbf{their}ID.}

· \textbf{Example:}\emph{Each of the players \textbf{has}submitted
\textbf{their}forms.}

· \textbf{Information booster:} Very formal styles may prefer \emph{his
or her}, but most contemporary exam boards and style guides accept
\textbf{singular they} for inclusivity and concision.

Q.119 \textbf{Identify the word that is not correctly spelled.}

A. Aberration\\
B. Discrepancy\\
C. Surveliance\\
D. Insurmountable

Answer: C

Sol: Option (c) is the \textbf{incorrectly} spelt word.

\textbf{Correct spelling is:} `` \textbf{Surveillance}'' --- meaning
\textbf{close observation} or \textbf{systematic monitoring} of
people/places/activities, often for security or research. \emph{(Hindi:}
निगरानी\emph{,} पर्यवेक्षण\emph{)} It derives from French \emph{sur} (over)
+ \emph{veiller} (to watch).

\textbf{Example:}\emph{The museum is under 24-hour surveillance to
protect the exhibits.}

\textbf{Meanings of all the given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Aberration:} a deviation from what is normal/expected; an
anomaly or departure from the usual course. \emph{(Hindi:}
सामान्यमार्गसेविचलन \emph{/} अपवाद\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(b) Discrepancy:} a lack of compatibility or similarity
between two or more facts; inconsistency. \emph{(Hindi:} असंगति \emph{/}
अंतर\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(c) Surveliance (}✗\textbf{) → Surveillance (}✓\textbf{):}
systematic observation/monitoring. \emph{(Hindi:} निगरानी \emph{/}
पर्यवेक्षण\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(d) Insurmountable:} too great to be overcome; impossible to
surmount. \emph{(Hindi:} अजेय \emph{/} दुर्जेय\emph{)}

Q.120 \textbf{Arrange the sentence parts:}\\
\textbf{(A) to submit (B) asked him (C) the report (D) the manager}

A. DBAC\\
B. BACD\\
C. DBCA\\
D. DCAB

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) is the correct order of the given sentences.

Explanation (why DBAC):\\
· D (the manager) provides the subject.

· B (asked him) supplies the finite verb + indirect object (``asked'' +
``him'').

· A (to submit) is the infinitive of purpose complementing ``asked
him''.

· C (the report) is the direct object of the infinitive ( \emph{submit
what? → the report}).

· Final sentence: ``The manager asked him to submit the report.''\\
· This respects standard S + V + IO + non-finite purpose + DO ordering
and yields a grammatical, natural English sentence.
\end{quote}

