Q.121 \textbf{Identify the antonym of
\textquotesingle Quell\textquotesingle.}

A. Suppress\\
B. Calm\\
C. Silence\\
D. Agitate

Answer: D

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (d) \textbf{Agitate}.

\textbf{Given word `Quell'}: To put an end to something by force; to
subdue or silence; also to pacify or allay (fear, unrest).
\emph{(Hindi:} दमनकरना\emph{/}शांतकरना\emph{/}थामदेना\emph{)}
\textbf{Example}: The police were deployed to \textbf{quell} the riots
before they spread.

\textbf{Correct answer word `Agitate'}: To stir up public feeling,
excite, or provoke; to disturb or make someone anxious. \emph{(Hindi:}
भड़काना\emph{/}उत्तेजितकरना\emph{/}व्याकुलकरना\emph{)} \textbf{Example}:
Irresponsible speeches can \textbf{agitate} the crowd and escalate
tensions.

\textbf{Synonyms (for `quell')}: suppress, subdue, quash, pacify.

\textbf{Antonyms (of `quell')}: agitate, incite, provoke, foment.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· (a) \textbf{Suppress}: To forcibly put an end to; to prevent
development. \emph{(Hindi:} दबाना\emph{/}रोकना\emph{)}\\
· (b) \textbf{Calm}: To make quiet or tranquil; free from agitation.
\emph{(Hindi:} शांतकरना\emph{/}शांत\emph{)}\\
· (c) \textbf{Silence}: To cause to be quiet; to stop from expressing.
\emph{(Hindi:} चुपकरादेना\emph{/}मौनकरदेना\emph{)}

Q.122 \textbf{Choose the correct one-word substitution for:}\\
\textbf{A place where aircraft are kept}

A. Dockyard\\
B. Depot\\
C. Hangar\\
D. Barracks

Answer: C

Sol: The correct one-word for the given group of words is (c)
\textbf{Hangar}.

\textbf{Explanation (}हिंदीअर्थ\textbf{):}\emph{Hangar} is a large
building/structure at an airport where aircraft are housed, serviced, or
repaired (विमान-शाला/विमान-भंडार). It protects aircraft from weather and
provides maintenance space.

\textbf{Example:} The engineers towed the jet into the \textbf{hangar}
for routine maintenance.

\textbf{Meanings of the given other options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Dockyard}: A place where ships are built, repaired, or
docked (जहाज़-गोदी/जहाज़-मरम्मतशाला).

· \textbf{(b) Depot}: A storage or transit station for goods/vehicles; a
warehouse or bus/rail base (गोदाम/डिपो).

· \textbf{(d) Barracks}: Buildings used to house soldiers
(सैनिकछावनी/बैरेक).

Q.123 \textbf{Find the antonym of
\textquotesingle Wilt\textquotesingle.}

A. Cruel\\
B. Merciless\\
C. Revive\\
D. Strict

Answer: C

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (c) \textbf{Revive}.

\textbf{Given word (`Wilt'):} To \textbf{wilt} means to become limp,
weak, or to lose freshness and vigor---commonly used for plants that
droop due to lack of water; figuratively, for people losing energy or
confidence.

\emph{(Hindi:} मुरझाना\emph{,} कुम्हलाजाना\emph{,} निर्बलपड़ना\emph{)}\\
\textbf{Example:} Without water for days, the leaves began to
\textbf{wilt} in the afternoon heat.

\textbf{Correct answer word (`Revive'):} To \textbf{revive} means to
restore life, strength, or consciousness; to become active or
flourishing again. \emph{(Hindi:} पुनर्जीवितकरना\emph{/}होना\emph{,}
स्फूर्तिलौटना\emph{)} \textbf{Example:} A little shade and water helped the
plant \textbf{revive} by evening.

\textbf{Synonyms (Wilt):} wither, droop, languish, flag.

\textbf{Antonyms (Wilt):} revive, flourish, bloom, perk up.

\textbf{Meanings of the other options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Cruel:} Willfully causing pain or suffering; unkind.
\emph{(Hindi:} क्रूर\emph{,} निर्दयी\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(b) Merciless:} Showing no mercy; pitiless. \emph{(Hindi:}
निर्दयी\emph{,} निष्ठुर\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(d) Strict:} Demanding that rules are obeyed; rigid.
\emph{(Hindi:} सख्त\emph{,} कठोर\emph{)}\\
These are qualities of behavior/attitude, not opposites of ``wilt.''

Q.124 \textbf{Change the following from active to passive:}\\
\textbf{People believe that the company will launch the new product
before the end of this fiscal year.}

A. The company believes to launch the product before the end of the
fiscal year.

B. The new product is believed to be launched before the fiscal year
ends.

C. It has been believed that the new product will launch this year.

D. It is believed that the company will launch the new product before
the end of this fiscal year.

Answer: D

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is (d).

\textbf{Explanation:} With impersonal subjects like \textbf{people
say/think/believe}, the most natural passive is the \textbf{impersonal
passive}: \textbf{It is believed that + clause}. We retain the future
construction \textbf{will launch} in the that-clause: \emph{It is
believed that the company will launch\ldots{}}\\
\textbf{Rules of conversion (believe/say/think type):}\\
\textbf{Active:} People + believe/think/say + \textbf{that} + S + V
\ldots{}\\
\textbf{Passive (impersonal):It is believed/ thought/ said that} + S + V
\ldots{}\\
\textbf{Alternate (personal) passive:} S (of that-clause) + \textbf{is
believed/ thought/ said} + \textbf{to + V} (tense adjustments needed).

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Active voice:}\emph{People believe that S + will + V1
\ldots{}}\\
· \textbf{Passive voice (impersonal):}\emph{It is believed that S + will
+ V1 \ldots{}}\\
\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (a) \emph{The company believes to launch\ldots{}} --- \textbf{believe
+ to-infinitive} is not used with \textbf{company} as the
\textbf{experiencer of its own action} here; also wrong re-casting of
the original meaning.

· (b) \emph{The new product is believed to be launched\ldots{}} ---
awkward/less idiomatic; the \textbf{to be launched} pattern suggests a
scheduled state; better is \emph{the company is believed to
launch\ldots{}} if using the personal passive.

· (c) \emph{It has been believed\ldots{}} --- present perfect passive
conveys \textbf{ongoing belief up to now}, which \textbf{alters the
aspect/meaning}; the original is a general present belief.

Q.125 \textbf{Select the synonym of Cacophony.}

A. Harmony\\
B. Discord\\
C. Order\\
D. Silence
\end{quote}

Copyright © 2026 Adda247

\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (b) \textbf{Discord}.

\textbf{Given word---Cacophony:} A harsh, jarring mixture of sounds;
unpleasant noise; dissonance (हिंदी: कर्णकटुध्वनि\textbf{/}विकटकोलाहल).\\
\textbf{Example:}\emph{The traffic created a cacophony that drowned out
our conversation.}

\textbf{Correct answer---Discord:} Lack of harmony; dissonance in sound
or agreement; a clash of tones or opinions (हिंदी:
असंगति\textbf{/}कलह\textbf{/}बे\textbf{-}सुरापन).\\
\textbf{Example:}\emph{Discord among the instruments made the rehearsal
difficult to endure.}

\textbf{Synonyms (for \emph{cacophony}):} discord, dissonance, din,
clamor.

\textbf{Antonyms:} harmony, euphony, melody, consonance.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Harmony:} a pleasing combination of sounds; agreement
(हिंदी: संगति\textbf{/}सामंजस्य).

· \textbf{(c) Order:} arrangement or regularity; absence of confusion
(हिंदी: व्यवस्था\textbf{/}क्रम).

· \textbf{(d) Silence:} complete absence of sound (हिंदी:
मौन\textbf{/}शांतता).

Q.126 \textbf{Choose the correct indirect speech form of the following
sentence.}\\
\textbf{They said, "They had a very good habit."}

A. They told that we had a very good habit.

B. They told that they had had a very good habit.

C. They told that they had a very good habit.\\
D. They told that we had had a very good habit.

Answer: B

Sol: The correct indirect/ direct speech is (b) They told that they had
had a very good habit.

Explanation: The reporting verb said (past) triggers backshifting in
reported speech. The original clause ``They had a very good habit'' is
simple past; on conversion it becomes past perfect → had had (first
\emph{had} = auxiliary; second \emph{had} = main verb ``possess''). The
pronoun they remains they since there's no change of viewpoint
indicated.

Rule highlights:\\
· Direct → Indirect: said/told + that + clause (backshift tense when
reporting verb is past).

· Simple Past → Past Perfect (e.g., \emph{had} → \emph{had had} when
``have'' is the main verb).

· Pronouns change only if the point of view/time/person changes.

Note: In strict grammar, told generally takes an object ( \emph{told
me/us}). However, among the given options, (b) alone preserves the
required tense backshift ( had had) and correct pronoun, so it is the
best/accepted answer here.

Why the other options are incorrect:\\
· (a) Changes pronoun to we (unwarranted) and keeps only had (no
backshift).

· (c) Keeps had (simple past) instead of had had (past perfect).

· (d) Uses we (incorrect shift of viewpoint), though had had is
tense-wise fine.

Q.127 \textbf{Select the correct passive form of the given sentence.}\\
\textbf{"The chef will prepare the meal by noon."}

A. The meal will be prepared by the chef by noon.\\
B. The chef will be prepared the meal.

C. The meal would be prepared by noon.

D. The meal will prepared by the chef by noon.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct passive voice of the given sentence is (a).

\textbf{Explain the rules of conversion from active to passive or
passive to active with structure in detail.}

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Active voice:} Subject + \textbf{will} + V₁ + Object +
(adverbials)\\
· \textbf{Passive voice:} Object + \textbf{will be} + V₃ + (by +
Subject) + (adverbials)\\
· Applying the rule:\\
· Active: \textbf{The chef} (S) + \textbf{will prepare} (V) +
\textbf{the meal} (O) + \textbf{by noon} (adverbial).

· Passive: \textbf{The meal} (O → S) + \textbf{will be prepared} (will +
be + past participle) + \textbf{by the chef} (agent) + \textbf{by noon}
(adverbial retained).\\
· Options (b) and (d) are ungrammatical in form; (c) changes the modal (
\textbf{would} ≠ \textbf{will}) and omits the agent and precise time
relation.

Q.128 \textbf{Which of the given options is the most logical sequencing
of the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph?}\\
\textbf{1. He has inspired millions through his speeches and writings.}

\textbf{2. He came to be known as the "Missile Man of India" for his
contributions.}\\
\textbf{3. In the year 2002, he became the President of India.}

\textbf{4. Dr. A.P .J. Abdul Kalam worked as a scientist at ISRO for
forty years.}

A. 1234\\
B. 4231\\
C. 4312\\
D. 2431

Answer: B

Sol: \textbf{Option (b) 4231} is the correct order of the given
sentences.

\textbf{Why this order is correct (chronology \& cohesion):}\\
· \textbf{4 → Career foundation:}\emph{``Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam worked
as a scientist at ISRO for forty years.''} --- Introduces the subject
and establishes his professional base.

· \textbf{2 → Epithet from contributions:} His ISRO work logically
\textbf{leads to} the title \emph{``Missile Man of India.''} The
nickname arises \textbf{because of} the contributions mentioned in (4).

· \textbf{3 → Peak public office:} After recognition, the narrative
moves to a milestone: \textbf{2002 Presidency}, which chronologically
follows his scientific acclaim.

· \textbf{1 → Lasting impact:} Concludes with his broader
\textbf{influence and inspiration} through speeches/writings---an effect
that spans post-Presidency and rounds off the paragraph with legacy.

\textbf{Why other orders fail:}\\
· \textbf{(a) 1234:} Starts with impact before introducing who he is or
why he's notable; lacks logical build-up.

· \textbf{(c) 4312:} Jumps from ISRO → Presidency → inspiration → title;
the epithet in (2) should precede (3) as it stems from scientific work.

· \textbf{(d) 2431:} Begins with the epithet without context, places
ISRO after it (reverse causality), then Presidency, then
inspiration---less coherent than 4231.

