Q.130 \textbf{Select the one-word term for: "Someone who is consistently
excluded or ignored by a community due to lack of trust or popularity."}

A. Dissenter\\
B. Noble\\
C. Pariah\\
D. Loyalist

Answer: C

Sol: The correct one-word for the given group of words is (c)
\textbf{Pariah}.

\textbf{Explanation: Pariah} means an outcast --- a person who is
socially shunned or ostracized by a community (बहिष्कृत/अछूत/तिरस्कृतव्यक्ति).
Such a person is excluded from normal social acceptance or privileges.

\textbf{Example:} After the scandal, he became a \textbf{pariah} in
political circles.

\textbf{Meanings of the given other options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Dissenter:} One who disagrees with the majority or
official opinion (असहमत/मतभेदरखनेवाला).

· \textbf{(b) Noble:} A person of high rank or admirable moral qualities
(कुलीन/उदार/महान).

· \textbf{(d) Loyalist:} A person who remains loyal to a cause, ruler,
or government (निष्ठावानसमर्थक/राजभक्त).

Q.131 \textbf{Choose the sentence that uses a homonym of the highlighted
word:}\\
\textbf{The lead pipes were replaced due to safety concerns.}

A. The general will lead the troops into battle.

B. Exposure to lead can cause health issues in children.\\
C. The old house still had lead in its plumbing system.\\
D. They tested the soil for traces of lead.

Answer: A

Sol: The sentence that uses a homonym is option (a) \textbf{lead}
(/liːd/, verb: ``to guide'')---a different word in meaning and
pronunciation from the highlighted \textbf{lead} (/lɛd/, noun: the metal
``सीसा''). \textbf{Explanation (detailed):} In the highlighted sentence,
\emph{lead} is a \textbf{noun} meaning a heavy, toxic metal used in old
pipes. In option (a), \emph{lead} is a \textbf{verb} meaning ``to
guide/command,'' a different lexical item that shares spelling (a
homograph) but differs in sound and meaning (a heteronym), hence a
homonymal use.

\textbf{Context note:} Options (b), (c), and (d) all use \emph{lead} as
the \textbf{metal}---same meaning as the highlighted word---so they do
\textbf{not} demonstrate the homonym.

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (b) \emph{lead} = the toxic \textbf{metal} (सीसा) --- same sense as
highlighted.

· (c) \emph{lead} = the \textbf{metal} in plumbing --- same sense as
highlighted.

· (d) \emph{lead} = testing soil for \textbf{metal} contamination ---
same sense as highlighted.

Q.132 \textbf{Choose the correct direct speech form of the following
sentence.}\\
\textbf{They said that they had already completed their homework.}

A. They said, "We already completed their homework."\\
B. They said, "We have completed our homework."\\
C. They said, "We have already completed homework of ours."\\
D. They said, "We have already completed our homework."

Answer: D

Sol: The correct indirect/ direct speech is (d).

\textbf{Explanation:} The indirect sentence uses \textbf{past perfect}
(``had already completed''), which normally backshifts from
\textbf{present perfect} in direct speech when the reporting verb is in
the past (``said''). While converting back to direct speech, we
\textbf{restore the original tense}: \emph{present perfect} → ``have
already completed''. \textbf{Pronoun shift} is also required: ``their''
(3rd person in reported speech) becomes \textbf{``our''} (1st person) to
reflect the original speakers (``They said, `We\ldots'\,''). The adverb
\textbf{``already''} remains as is.

\textbf{Rules of conversion (key points):}\\
· Reporting verb in past → backshift in indirect (e.g., \emph{have
completed} → \emph{had completed}). Reverse this when restoring direct
speech.

· Change of \textbf{person}: 3rd person ``their'' → 1st person ``our''
to match the original speaker.

· \textbf{Punctuation} in direct speech: comma after reporting clause +
quotation marks around the reported clause.

· \textbf{Example (rule):} Indirect: \emph{She said that she had
finished her work.} → Direct: \emph{She said, ``I have finished my
work.''}\\
\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (a) ``their'' is wrong; speakers would say \textbf{our}. Also
``already completed their homework'' mismatches person reference.

· (b) Missing \textbf{``already''}, which is present in the original
meaning.

· (c) Awkward/unidiomatic phrasing ``homework of ours''; standard is
\textbf{our homework}.

Q.133 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence.}\\
\textbf{I am not used with this kind of work.}

A. to this kind of work\\
B. about this kind of work\\
C. at this kind of work\\
D. for this kind of work

Answer: A

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (a) \textbf{to
this kind of work}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The expression is \textbf{be used to +
noun/gerund}, meaning ``be accustomed to.'' Here, ``this kind of work''
is a noun phrase, so the correct preposition is \textbf{to}, not
``with.'' Hence, ``I am not used \textbf{to} this kind of work.''\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{used to + V1} = past habit/state (e.g., I used to play).

· \textbf{be used to + noun/gerund} = be accustomed to (e.g., I am used
to playing/heat/noise).

· \textbf{Example:}\\
· She \textbf{is used to} early mornings. / He \textbf{is not used to}
speaking in public.

· \textbf{Information booster / exceptions:} After \textbf{be used to},
you can place either a noun ( \textbf{noise}) or a gerund (
\textbf{working}). Do \textbf{not} confuse it with \textbf{used to + V1}
indicating an earlier habit that no longer continues.

Q.134 \textbf{Which of the given options is the most logical sequencing
of the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph?}\\
\textbf{1. Franklin hypothesized that lightning was a form of electrical
discharge.}

\textbf{2. Franklin flew a kite made of a silk string and attached a
metal key to it during a storm.}

\textbf{3. He wanted to prove this by conducting an experiment to
capture the electrical charge from a thunderstorm.}\\
\textbf{4. As the storm approached, the kite was struck by lightning,
and the metal key conducted the electrical charge.}

A. 1-2-3-4\\
B. 1-3-2-4 Copyright © 2026 Adda247\\
C. 1-2-4-3

\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

Answer: B

Sol: Option \textbf{(b)} is the correct order of the given sentences.

· \textbf{1} states the \textbf{hypothesis} (lightning = electrical
discharge), which naturally precedes any action.

· \textbf{3} expresses the \textbf{purpose/plan} to test the hypothesis
(``He wanted to prove this by conducting an experiment\ldots'').

· \textbf{2} provides the \textbf{method} of the experiment (flying a
kite with a metal key during a storm).

· \textbf{4} gives the \textbf{result/outcome} (lightning strikes;
charge is conducted), completing a coherent scientific narrative:
\textbf{hypothesis → intent → method → result (1-3-2-4).}

Q.135 \textbf{Arrange the sentence parts:}\\
\textbf{(A) should follow (B) traffic rules (C) strictly (D) everyone}

A. DABC\\
B. DCAB\\
C. DBCA\\
D. DACB

Answer: A

Sol: Option \textbf{(a)} is the correct order of the given sentences.

· \textbf{D (everyone)} provides the \textbf{subject}.

· \textbf{A (should follow)} gives the \textbf{modal + main verb}.

· \textbf{B (traffic rules)} is the \textbf{object} of the verb
``follow.''\\
· \textbf{C (strictly)} is an \textbf{adverb} modifying how the action
should be done.

· Final sentence: \textbf{``Everyone should follow traffic rules
strictly.''}\\
· This follows the natural English order \textbf{Subject → Modal/Verb →
Object → Adverbial}.

Q.136 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence.}\\
\textbf{He has much works to finish.}

A. a lot of work\\
B. plenty works\\
C. many works\\
D. lot of tasks

Answer: A

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (a) \textbf{a
lot of work}.

\textbf{Explanation:} ``Work'' in the sense of tasks/assignments is an
\textbf{uncountable noun}, so it does \textbf{not} take a plural
``works.''\\
Also, in affirmative sentences, \textbf{a lot of} is the most natural
quantifier with uncountable nouns (``a lot of work'').

``Much work'' is grammatically possible but sounds formal/stiff in
everyday usage; the error in the original is the \textbf{plural}
``works,'' not just the quantifier.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:} Uncountable nouns (advice, furniture,
information, luggage, homework, work) don't take plural forms and pair
with quantifiers like \textbf{much/a lot of/plenty of} (not numbers).
\textbf{Example:} She has \textbf{a lot of homework}, so she can't come
out tonight.

\textbf{Info booster / exceptions:}\\
· \textbf{Works} is correct when it means artistic/literary creations
(``the collected \textbf{works} of Shakespeare'') or industrial
facilities (``iron \textbf{works}''). It is \textbf{not} used for
everyday ``tasks.''· In negative/interrogatives, \textbf{much} is
common: ``He doesn't have \textbf{much work} left,'' ``Do you have
\textbf{much work}?''

Q.137 \textbf{Choose the most suitable option to replace the highlighted
part of the sentence. If the sentence is already correct, select
\textquotesingle No substitution required\textquotesingle. \emph{If I
was you} , I would consult a specialist.}

A. No substitution required\\
B. am you\\
C. would be you\\
D. were you

Answer: D

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (d)
\textbf{were you}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The sentence expresses an unreal/hypothetical
condition (I am not you). In such \emph{subjunctive} or contrary-to-fact
conditionals, English uses \textbf{were} with all persons after
\emph{if} (If I \textbf{were}\ldots, If he \textbf{were}\ldots, etc.).
Hence, ``If I \textbf{were} you, I would consult a specialist'' is the
grammatically correct, idiomatic structure.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{Subjunctive mood in unreal conditionals}: \emph{If + subject +
\textbf{were}, subject + would + V₁}.

· With the verb \textbf{be}, the form \textbf{were} is used for all
persons to indicate unreality/hypothesis.

\textbf{Example:}\emph{If she \textbf{were}here, we \textbf{would}start
immediately.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· Other triggers for the past subjunctive \textbf{were}: \emph{wish, if
only, as if/as though} (e.g., \emph{I wish he \textbf{were}taller}).

Q.138 \textbf{Choose the correct direct speech form of the following
sentence. He told that when they had reached the station, the train had
already left.}

A. He said, "When we have reach the station, the train had already
left."\\
B. He said, "When we reached the station, the train had already left."\\
C. He said, "When we were reaching the station, the train was already
leaving."\\
D. He said, "When we reach the station, the train has already left."

Answer: B

Sol: The correct indirect/ direct speech is (b).

\textbf{Explanation:} Turning the reported statement into direct speech
requires restoring the original wording and correct tense sequence. The
time relation is: \textbf{reaching} happened first; \textbf{leaving} was
already completed. Hence in direct speech: \textbf{simple past} in the
time clause (``we reached'') + \textbf{past perfect} in the main clause
(``had already left''). Pronoun shift \textbf{they → we} is natural
because the speaker includes himself. The reporting verb ``said'' is
standard for introducing direct quotes.

\textbf{Rules of conversion (Indirect → Direct):}\\
· Replace the conjunction \emph{that} with quotation marks after a
suitable reporting verb (often \emph{said}).

· Adjust \textbf{pronouns} to reflect the original speaker's viewpoint
(they → we).

· Maintain \textbf{sequence of tenses} to preserve time relations:
earlier past action → \textbf{past perfect}; later/point past action →
\textbf{simple past}.

\textbf{Why others are incorrect:}\\
· (a) ``have reach'' → wrong verb form; also tense mismatch.

· (c) Progressive pair (``were reaching/was leaving'') changes the
meaning to simultaneous ongoing actions, not a completed-before
relation.

· (d) Present tenses (``reach/has left'') misrepresent past-time context
and sequence.

