\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

e medications?"\\
C. He said, "Did I take the medications?"\\
D. He said, "Did you took the medications?"

Answer: B

Sol: The correct indirect/ direct speech is (b).

\textbf{Explanation:} A \textbf{yes/no} indirect question with
\textbf{whether/if + past perfect (had taken)} often backshifts from a
\textbf{simple past question} in direct speech: \textbf{``Did you
take\ldots?'' → \ldots{} whether I had taken\ldots{}}. When restoring to
direct speech:\\
· Use \textbf{question word order} with auxiliary \textbf{did}:
\textbf{Did you take \ldots?}

· Keep correct \textbf{pronouns}: \emph{you → I} (in indirect) and
\textbf{back to}\emph{you} (in direct).

· Maintain \textbf{simple past} ( \emph{did take}) rather than incorrect
forms.

\textbf{Rules of conversion (Questions):}\\
· \textbf{Direct → Indirect (yes/no):} reporting verb (asked) +
\textbf{if/whether} + \textbf{subject + tense} (backshift if reporting
verb past) + \textbf{no question order}.

· \textbf{Indirect → Direct:} re-introduce \textbf{question order} and
\textbf{auxiliary} (Do/Did/Has\ldots), restore \textbf{pronouns/time} to
original perspective.

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (a) Uses \textbf{``Whether''} inside direct question ---
\textbf{unidiomatic}; we use \textbf{if/whether} in indirect, not in
direct yes/no questions.

· (c) Changes pronoun to \textbf{I} --- alters the \textbf{speaker's
role}; wrong sense.

· (d) \textbf{``Did you took''} --- tense error (auxiliary \textbf{did}
requires base verb \textbf{take}, not \textbf{took}).

Q.140 \textbf{Select the synonym of Pinnacle.}

A. Base\\
B. Decline\\
C. Bottom\\
D. Peak

Answer: D

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (d) \textbf{Peak}.

\textbf{Given word `Pinnacle'}: The highest point of development or
achievement; a summit or acme. \emph{(Hindi:}
शिखर\emph{/}चरमबिंदु\emph{)}\\
\textbf{Example}: Winning the international award was the
\textbf{pinnacle} of her career.

\textbf{Correct answer word `Peak'}: The topmost point or maximum level
of something; summit. \emph{(Hindi:} शिखर\emph{/}चोटी\emph{)}\\
\textbf{Example}: The athlete reached the \textbf{peak} of his
performance in the final match.

\textbf{Synonyms}: apex, acme, zenith, summit.

\textbf{Antonyms}: nadir, base, bottom, low point.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· (a) \textbf{Base}: Lowest part or foundation of something.
\emph{(Hindi:} आधार\emph{/}निचलाभाग\emph{)}\\
· (b) \textbf{Decline}: A gradual loss or deterioration. \emph{(Hindi:}
गिरावट\emph{/}पतन\emph{)}\\
· (c) \textbf{Bottom}: The lowest point or part. \emph{(Hindi:}
तल\emph{/}सबसेनीचेकाभाग\emph{)}

Q.141 \textbf{Select the correct spelling of a word meaning
\textquotesingle a long adventurous journey\textquotesingle.}

A. Odessay\\
B. Odyssey\\
C. Odessy\\
D. Oddysey

Answer: B

Sol: Option (b) is the correctly spelt word.

Correct spelling is: `` Odyssey'' --- It means a long, adventurous
journey, often filled with notable experiences or challenges; it can
also refer to a series of wanderings marked by many changes of fortune.
(Hindi: \textbf{लंबीसाहसिकयात्रा}, \textbf{रोमांचकभ्रमण})\\
Example: After quitting his job, Raghav began a year-long odyssey across
the mountains of Central Asia.

Q.142 \textbf{Select the synonym of Obsequious.}

A. Defiant\\
B. Aggressive\\
C. Proud\\
D. Submissive

Answer: D

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (d) \textbf{Submissive}.

\textbf{Obsequious}: showing excessive eagerness to please; servile,
fawning, sycophantic in order to gain favor. (Hindi: चापलूस \textbf{/}
खुशामदी \textbf{/} दासवत).\\
\textbf{Example:}\emph{The manager surrounded himself with
\textbf{obsequious}assistants who agreed with everything he said.}

\textbf{Submissive}: inclined or ready to submit to authority; meekly
obedient or compliant. (Hindi: आज्ञाकारी \textbf{/} विनम्र \textbf{/}
आत्मसमर्पी).

\textbf{Example:}\emph{Her \textbf{submissive}tone suggested she would
follow the instructions without protest.}

\textbf{Synonyms:} servile, fawning, sycophantic, subservient.

\textbf{Antonyms:} assertive, defiant, independent, domineering (as an
opposite tendency).

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Defiant:} boldly resisting authority or opposition.
(Hindi: अवज्ञाकारी \textbf{/} उद्दंड).

· \textbf{(b) Aggressive:} ready or likely to attack; forceful, pushy.
(Hindi: आक्रामक).

· \textbf{(c) Proud:} having a high opinion of oneself; feeling deep
pleasure or satisfaction. (Hindi: गर्वित \textbf{/} अभिमानी).

Q.143 \textbf{Identify the part containing a grammatical or structural
error:}\\
\textbf{The behaviour of these students leave much to be desired.}

A. leave much\\
B. The behaviour\\
C. of these students\\
D. to be desired

Answer: A

Sol: Option \textbf{(a)} contains an error.

\textbf{Detailed explanation:} The \textbf{subject} is \textbf{``The
behaviour''} (singular, uncountable), so the verb must be
\textbf{singular}: \textbf{``leaves,''} not ``leave.''\\
The prepositional phrase \textbf{``of these students''} does not affect
subject-verb agreement. Correct sentence: \textbf{``The behaviour of
these students leaves much to be desired.''}\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used:} In \textbf{Subject + of + Noun}
structures, the verb agrees with the \textbf{head noun before ``of''}
(here, \textbf{behaviour}). Uncountable/singular nouns take
\textbf{singular} verbs (behaviour \textbf{leaves}, furniture
\textbf{is}, information \textbf{is}).

\textbf{Example:}\emph{The quality of the apples \textbf{is}excellent.}
( \emph{quality} = head, singular)\\
\textbf{Info booster / exceptions:} Be careful with \textbf{collective
nouns} (team, committee): singular in British English unless emphasizing
individuals; but \textbf{behaviour} is unequivocally
singular/uncountable in this sense and requires a singular verb.
\end{quote}

