Q.144 \textbf{Select the correct active form of the given sentence.}\\
\textbf{"The hall is being decorated by the staff."}

A. The staff is decorating the hall.

B. The staff is being decorated the hall.\\
C. The staff decorate the hall.

D. The hall is decorating the staff.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is
\textbf{(a) The staff is decorating the hall.}

\textbf{Explain the rules of conversion (Passive → Active) with
structure (in detail):}\\
· Identify the tense/aspect in the passive: \textbf{is being decorated}
= \textbf{Present Continuous Passive} ( \emph{am/is/are + being + V³}).

· Move the agent (the phrase after \textbf{by}) to the subject position:
\textbf{by the staff} → \textbf{The staff} (subject).

· Keep the same tense/aspect in active: \textbf{Present Continuous
Active} ( \emph{am/is/are + V-ing}).

· Retain the original object after the verb: \textbf{the hall}.

· Ensure subject--verb agreement: with the collective noun
\textbf{staff}, exams commonly accept singular agreement when treated as
a single unit → \textbf{is decorating}. (Note: In British usage,
\textbf{staff} can also be plural--- \emph{The staff are\ldots{}}---but
you must select from the given options; (a) matches tense and structure
correctly.)\\
\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Passive Voice (Present Continuous):}\emph{Object} + am/is/are
+ \textbf{being} + V³ + (by + \emph{Agent})\\
· \emph{The hall}\textbf{is being decorated}\emph{(by the staff)}.

· \textbf{Active Voice (Present Continuous):}\emph{Subject (Agent)} +
am/is/are + V-ing + \emph{Object}\\
· \emph{The staff}\textbf{is decorating}\emph{the hall}.

\textbf{Example (parallel):}\\
· Passive: \emph{The rooms \textbf{are being cleaned}by the
housekeepers.}

· Active: \emph{The housekeepers \textbf{are cleaning}the rooms.}

\textbf{Why the other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(b) The staff is being decorated the hall.} --- Ungrammatical
hybrid: mixes passive auxiliary ( \textbf{is being}) with an active
object; correct active should be \textbf{is decorating}.

· \textbf{(c) The staff decorate the hall.} --- \textbf{Simple Present}
(habitual), not \textbf{Present Continuous}; changes the aspect and does
not reflect the passive ``is being\ldots''.

· \textbf{(d) The hall is decorating the staff.} --- Reverses semantic
roles (nonsensical) and changes meaning.

Q.145 \textbf{Choose the correct indirect speech form of the following
sentence.}\\
\textbf{Rajesh says to Malini, "We did not like the dance last year."}

A. Rajesh tells Malini that they had not liked the dance the previous
year.\\
B. Rajesh tells Malini that they did not like the dance the previous
year.\\
C. Rajesh told Malini that they did not like the dance last year.

D. Rajesh told Malini that they had not liked the dance the previous
year.

Answer: B

Sol: The correct indirect/ direct speech is (b).

Explanation: The reporting verb is in the present (``says to''), so we
generally do not backshift the tense of the reported clause. ``Did not
like'' (simple past) remains did not like. Also, ``says to'' →``tells''
in indirect speech. Pronoun we → they (as the reporter is a third person
and ``we'' does not include the reporter). Time phrase ``last year'' may
remain or be adapted to ``the previous year'' for clarity---both are
acceptable with a present reporting verb; the option uses the adapted
form, which is fine.

Rules of conversion (Statement):\\
· say to → tell (say to + person is re-expressed as tell + person).

· Keep tense when reporting verb is present: Present reporting → no
backshift.

· Adjust pronouns/time/place as per context.

· Quotation marks removed; that-clause used.

Why other options are incorrect:\\
· (a) Uses had not liked (past perfect) --- an unnecessary backshift
with a present reporting verb.

· (c) Changes says to told and keeps last year; though formable in
another context, it doesn't match the given present reporting verb.

· (d) Changes reporting verb to past and backshifts --- again not
aligned with the original.

Q.146 \textbf{Fill in the blank with an appropriate adverb.}\\
\textbf{The food is \_\_\_ good.}

A. cautiously\\
B. extremely\\
C. boldly\\
D. annually

Answer: B

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is \textbf{(b) extremely}.

`` \textbf{Extremely}'' is a degree adverb that intensifies adjectives
(e.g., \emph{extremely good}, \emph{extremely cold}). Here it modifies
the adjective ``good'' to express a high degree of quality.
\emph{(Hindi:} अत्यंत\emph{/}बेहद\emph{)} \textbf{Example:} The dessert was
\textbf{extremely} good.

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(a) cautiously:} Manner adverb modifying verbs (e.g.,
\emph{drive cautiously}), not degree of an adjective. \emph{(Hindi:}
सावधानीपूर्वक\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(c) boldly:} Manner adverb describing the way an action is
performed (e.g., \emph{speak boldly}). \emph{(Hindi:}
निडरतासे\emph{/}साहसपूर्वक\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(d) annually:} Frequency/time adverb (once a year), unsuitable
before an adjective like ``good.'' \emph{(Hindi:} वार्षिकरूपसे\emph{)}

Q.147 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to fill in the
blank.}\\
\textbf{He is good\_\_\_\_\_ mathematics.}

A. by\\
B. to\\
C. in\\
D. at

Answer: D

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (d) \textbf{at}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The fixed collocation is \textbf{good at +
subject/skill} (good at maths, good at chess, good at cooking). It
expresses competence or proficiency. (Hindi: गणितमेंअच्छा \textbf{/} निपुण).
\textbf{Example:}\emph{She is \textbf{good at}mathematics and often
helps her friends with calculus.}

\textbf{Explain why other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(a) by:} indicates agency/instrument (``by train,'' ``by
him''); doesn't collocate with ``good''. (Hindi: द्वारा).

· \textbf{(b) to:} marks direction/recipient (``to the park,'' ``good to
someone'' = kind); not for ability. (Hindi: को \textbf{/} तक).

· \textbf{(c) in:} sometimes used for performance within a context (
\emph{good in class/exams}), but with a \textbf{subject/skill}, standard
usage is \textbf{good at}. (Hindi: में).

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· \textbf{good at} + activity/subject/tool ( \emph{good at coding /
algebra / the violin}).

· \textbf{good with} + instruments/people ( \emph{good with children /
numbers}).

· \textbf{good in} + context/situation ( \emph{good in interviews / in
class}).
\end{quote}

