Q.154 \textbf{Choose the antonym of
\textquotesingle magnanimous\textquotesingle:}

A. Noble\\
B. Petty\\
C. Forgiving\\
D. Generous

Answer: B

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (b) \textbf{Petty}.

\textbf{Magnanimous}: A person who is large-hearted, generous in
forgiving insults or injuries; noble in mind and heart; free from
pettiness (Hindi: उदार\textbf{,} महान\textbf{-}हृदय\textbf{,} क्षमाशील).
\textbf{Example:}\emph{Despite the harsh criticism, she remained
\textbf{magnanimous}and even praised her opponent's efforts.}

\textbf{Correct answer word --- Petty:} Small-minded, mean-spirited,
concerned with trivial or spiteful matters; ungenerous (Hindi:
छोटा\textbf{-}दिल\textbf{,} क्षुद्र\textbf{,} संकीर्ण).

\textbf{Example:}\emph{It is \textbf{petty}to hold a grudge over such a
minor mistake.}

\textbf{Synonyms (of magnanimous):} generous, noble, benevolent,
big-hearted.

\textbf{Antonyms (of magnanimous):} petty, mean, grudging, small-minded.

\textbf{Meanings of other options:}\\
· (a) \textbf{Noble}: high-minded, morally elevated (Hindi:
उच्च\textbf{,} महान).

· (c) \textbf{Forgiving}: willing to pardon; not resentful (Hindi:
क्षमाशील).

· (d) \textbf{Generous}: liberal in giving; kind (Hindi: उदार).

Q.155 \textbf{Choose the correct indirect speech form of the following
sentence.}\\
\textbf{Roshan says, "I have completed my homework."}

A. Roshan said that he had completed his homework.\\
B. Roshan says that he had completed his homework.\\
C. Roshan says that he has completed his homework.\\
D. Roshan said that he has completed his homework.

Answer: C

Sol: The correct indirect speech is \textbf{(c) Roshan says that he has
completed his homework.}

\textbf{Explanation}: The reporting verb \textbf{says} is in the
\textbf{simple present}, so there is \textbf{no backshift} of tense. The
first person pronoun \textbf{I} changes to \textbf{he} to match the
subject \textbf{Roshan}. The present perfect \textbf{have completed}
remains \textbf{has completed} (agreement with \textbf{he}). Quotation
marks are removed and \textbf{that} is inserted.

\textbf{Rules of conversion (Direct → Indirect when reporting verb is
present):}\\
· If the reporting verb is \textbf{in present/future}, the \textbf{tense
of the reported speech generally remains the same}.

· \textbf{Pronouns} change according to person and number to maintain
reference.

· \textbf{Punctuation}: remove commas/quotes; introduce \textbf{that}
for statements.

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Direct}: S + say/says + `` + S' + have/has + V₃ + \ldots{} +
''\\
· \textbf{Indirect}: S + say/says + that + S'\,' + has/have + V₃ +
\ldots{}\\
\textbf{Example:}\\
· Direct: She says, ``I have finished the project.''\\
· Indirect: She says that she has finished the project.

\textbf{Note (Backshift when reporting verb is past):}\\
· If it were \textbf{Roshan said, ``I have completed my homework,''} the
correct indirect form would be \textbf{Roshan said that he had completed
his homework.}

Q.156 \textbf{Select the correct active form of the given sentence. "The
letters were delivered by the postman."}

A. The postman had delivered the letters.\\
B. The postman delivers the letters.

C. The postman has delivered the letters.

D. The postman delivered the letters.

Answer: D

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is (d)
\textbf{The postman delivered the letters.}

\textbf{Explain the rules of conversion (Passive → Active) with
structure in detail:}\\
· \textbf{Identify tense:} ``were delivered'' = \textbf{Simple Past
Passive}.

· \textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Passive Voice:}\emph{Object} + \textbf{was/were} + V3 + (by +
\emph{Agent})\\
· ``The letters \textbf{were delivered} (by the postman).''\\
· \textbf{Active Voice:}\emph{Subject} + V2 + \emph{Object}\\
· ``The postman \textbf{delivered} the letters.''\\
· \textbf{Steps:} Move the agent (``the postman'') to subject position,
keep the tense as \textbf{Simple Past} (V2 = delivered), and place the
original subject (``the letters'') as the object.

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(a) had delivered} = Past Perfect, not Simple Past.

· \textbf{(b) delivers} = Simple Present, tense change.

· \textbf{(c) has delivered} = Present Perfect, tense change.

Q.157 \textbf{What does the idiom \textquotesingle Take to
task\textquotesingle{} mean?}

A. Scold\\
B. Congratulate\\
C. Reward\\
D. Praise

Answer: A

Sol: \textbf{Option (a)} is the correct meaning of the given idiom.

\textbf{Given Idiom: `Take to task'} --- To reprimand or \textbf{scold}
someone for a fault or mistake; to call them to account. \emph{(Hindi:}
कड़ीफटकारलगाना\emph{,} अनुशासनात्मकरूपसेटोकना\emph{)} \textbf{Example:} The
manager \textbf{took the team to task} for missing the deadline.

