Q.158 \textbf{Choose the correct indirect speech form of the following
sentence.}

\textbf{Rashmi said to Reshma, "Mahima was dancing on the floor when she
was called by her mother."}

A. Rashmi told Reshma that Mahima had danced on the floor when she had
been called by her mother.

B. Rashmi told Reshma that Mahima had been danching on the floor when
was called by her mother.\\
C. Rashmi told Reshma that Mahima had been dancing on the floor when she
had been called by her mother.\\
D. Rashmi told Reshma that Mahima was dancing on the floor when she was
called by her mother.

Answer: C

Sol: The correct indirect speech is (c).

Explanation of conversion:\\
· Reporting verb: \emph{said to} → told (add an object).

· Conjunction: introduce that.

· Backshift of tenses (since reporting verb is in the past):\\
· Past Continuous ( \emph{was dancing}) → Past Perfect Continuous (
\emph{had been dancing}).

· Simple Past Passive ( \emph{was called}) → Past Perfect Passive (
\emph{had been called}).

· Pronouns/Time: remain consistent; \emph{she} continues to refer to
Mahima in context.

· Hence: Rashmi told Reshma that Mahima had been dancing on the floor
when she had been called by her mother.

Rules of conversion (Direct → Indirect):\\
· \emph{say to + person} → tell + person; \emph{say that} → possible
when no indirect object.

· Backshift: Present → Past; Past → Past Perfect; Past Continuous → Past
Perfect Continuous; \emph{was/were + V3} → \emph{had been + V3} in
passive.\\
· Keep logical sequence of tenses across main and subordinate clauses
introduced by when/while.

Structure:\\
· Direct: S + said to + O, ``S + was V-ing \ldots{} when S + was V3
\ldots.''\\
· Indirect: S + told + O + that + S + had been V-ing \ldots{} when S +
had been V3 \ldots.

Example:\\
Direct: \emph{He said, ``I was reading when the lights went out.''}\\
Indirect: \emph{He said that he had been reading when the lights had
gone out.}

Q.159 \textbf{Select the most appropriate synonym of the given word.}\\
\textbf{Delegate}

A. nominate\\
B. detain\\
C. create\\
D. possess

Answer: A

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (a) \textbf{nominate}.

\textbf{Word Meaning:}\\
\textbf{Delegate} (verb): to assign responsibility or authority to
another person (किसीकोकामसौंपना)\\
It can also be a noun: a person chosen or elected to represent others.

Example (verb): The manager delegated the task to his assistant.

Example (noun): She is a delegate to the UN conference.

\textbf{Synonym: Nominate -- to appoint someone to a duty or position
(}नामितकरना\textbf{)}\\
Example: He was nominated to represent the company at the conference.

\textbf{Synonyms:} assign, authorize, designate, nominate.

\textbf{Antonyms:} retain, hold, deny, keep.

\textbf{Meanings of Other}\\
· (b) \textbf{Detain}: to hold someone back, often legally (रोकना)\\
· (c) \textbf{Create}: to make or bring into existence (बनाना)\\
· (d) \textbf{Possess}: to own or have (स्वामित्वहोना)\\
Only ``nominate'' aligns with the idea of delegating authority or
responsibility.

Q.160 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to fill in the
blank.}\\
\textbf{The police looked \_\_\_\_\_the matter.}

A. into\\
B. for\\
C. by\\
D. at

Answer: A

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (a) \textbf{into}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The phrasal verb \textbf{look into} means ``to
investigate or examine carefully'' --- exactly what the police do with a
case or matter. (Hindi: जाँचकरना, गहराईसेजांचना) \textbf{Example:} The
committee will \textbf{look into} the allegations before taking action.

\textbf{Explain why other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(b) for}: \textbf{look for} = to search for something/someone
(Hindi: खोजना); not used with ``the matter.''\\
· \textbf{(c) by}: \textbf{look by} is not a standard phrasal verb
(Hindi: प्रचलितप्रयोगनहीं).

· \textbf{(d) at}: \textbf{look at} = to direct one's eyes toward;
sometimes ``consider,'' but with ``the matter'' in police context,
\textbf{look into} (investigate) is precise (Hindi: देखना/विचारकरना,
परजाँचनहीं).

