Q.162 \textbf{Choose the correct option to complete the sentence.}\\
\textbf{She tied the belt tightly around her \_\_\_\_\_\_\_ .}

A. waste\\
B. weist\\
C. west\\
D. waist

Answer: D

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (d) \textbf{waist}.

\textbf{Explanation:}\emph{Waist} means the \textbf{part of the human
body between the ribs and hips} (Hindi: कमर). The sentence speaks of
tying a belt, which naturally goes around the \textbf{waist}, not
``waste/west.''\textbf{Example:}\emph{The tailor measured her
\textbf{waist}before stitching the dress.}

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (a) \textbf{waste}: unwanted material; misuse/spend carelessly (Hindi:
कचरा\textbf{/}अपव्यय).

· (b) \textbf{weist}: non-standard/incorrect spelling; no valid common
English word for this context.

· (c) \textbf{west}: direction opposite east (Hindi: पश्चिम).

Q.163 \textbf{Convert the sentence provided below from its passive voice
structure to an active voice structure:}\\
\textbf{The house is cleaned by her every morning before breakfast.}

A. She is cleaning the house every morning before breakfast.

B. She cleaned the house every morning before breakfast.\\
C. She cleans the house every morning before breakfast.

D. She was cleaning the house every morning before breakfast.

Answer: C

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is (c)
\textbf{She cleans the house every morning before breakfast.}

\textbf{Explain the rules of conversion (Passive → Active) with
structure:}\\
· Identify the tense/aspect in the passive sentence. Here: \textbf{is
cleaned} = \textbf{Simple Present Passive} (am/is/are + V³).

· Move the \textbf{agent} after \emph{by} to the subject position and
change the \textbf{object pronoun} to \textbf{subject pronoun}:
\emph{her} → \textbf{she}.

· Keep the \textbf{tense} the same when converting: Simple Present
Passive → \textbf{Simple Present Active}.

· Maintain \textbf{adverbials of frequency/time} (``every morning before
breakfast'') in the sentence; their position can remain at the end (or
before the main verb in some styles) but meaning must stay intact.·
Ensure \textbf{subject--verb agreement} in simple present: third person
singular → verb + \textbf{s/es} ( \textbf{cleans}).

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Passive Voice (Simple Present):}\emph{Object} + am/is/are + V³
+ (by + \emph{Agent}) + Adverbials\\
· ``The house \textbf{is cleaned} (by her) every morning before
breakfast.''\\
· \textbf{Active Voice (Simple Present):}\emph{Subject} + V¹(s/es) +
\emph{Object} + Adverbials\\
· `` \textbf{She cleans} the house every morning before breakfast.''\\
\textbf{Example (parallel):}\\
· Passive: ``The room \textbf{is vacuumed} by the attendant daily.''\\
· Active: ``The attendant \textbf{vacuums} the room daily.''\\
\textbf{Why the other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(a) She is cleaning\ldots{}} → \textbf{Present Continuous} (is
+ V-ing) expresses an ongoing action, not a \textbf{habitual} action.
The adverbial ``every morning'' signals habit; use \textbf{simple
present}.

· \textbf{(b) She cleaned\ldots{}} → \textbf{Simple Past}, which shifts
the time frame to finished past actions; the original shows a
\textbf{present habitual} routine.

· \textbf{(d) She was cleaning\ldots{}} → \textbf{Past Continuous},
again mismatching the tense/aspect and the habitual meaning.

\textbf{Quick tip:} For habitual truths/routines signaled by adverbs
like \textbf{always, often, every day/morning}, prefer \textbf{Simple
Present} in active voice; in passive, this maps to \textbf{am/is/are +
V³}.

Q.164 \textbf{Find the incorrect part of the sentence:}\\
\textbf{She is junior than me.}

A. She is\\
B. junior than\\
C. No error\\
D. me

Answer: B

Sol: Option (b) contains an error.

\textbf{Detailed explanation of error:} After adjectives of comparison
denoting \textbf{rank/status/quality}---such as \textbf{senior, junior,
superior, inferior, prior, posterior, anterior, preferable}---the
preposition \textbf{``to''} (not``than'') is used. Hence, it should be
\textbf{``She is junior to me.''}\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· Certain adjectives take \textbf{preposition ``to''} rather than
``than'': \emph{senior to, junior to, superior to, inferior to, prior
to, preferable to}.

· When comparing persons by position/rank, we do \textbf{not} use
``than.''\\
\textbf{Example:}\\
· \emph{Rita is senior \textbf{to}me in this department.}

· \emph{This method is preferable \textbf{to}that one.}

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· Note that \textbf{``elder/older''} typically takes \textbf{``than''} (
\emph{She is older than me}), but \textbf{``senior/junior''} take
\textbf{``to.''}

Q.165 \textbf{Choose the antonym of
\textquotesingle tangible\textquotesingle:}

A. Invisible\\
B. Real\\
C. Intangible\\
D. Definite

Answer: C

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (c) \textbf{Intangible}.

\textbf{Given word---Tangible:} Something that can be touched or
perceived by the senses; real, concrete, and measurable (हिंदी:
स्पृश्य\textbf{/}मूर्त\textbf{/}ठोस).

\textbf{Example:}\emph{We need tangible results to justify the
investment.}

\textbf{Correct answer---Intangible:} Something that cannot be touched
physically; lacking physical substance; abstract; not easily measured
(हिंदी: अमूर्त\textbf{/}अस्पृश्य). \textbf{Example:}\emph{Trust is an
intangible asset for any organization.}
\end{quote}

