Q.166 \textbf{Choose the correct sequence to form a meaningful
sentence:}\\
\textbf{(A) very interesting (B) is reading (C) books (D) fiction}

A. BDCA\\
B. BADC\\
C. BCAD\\
D. BACD

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) is the correct order of the given sentences.

· Sequence \textbf{BDCA} → \textbf{``Is reading fiction books very
interesting?''}\\
· \textbf{B (``is reading'')} provides the auxiliary \textbf{is} placed
before the subject to form a \textbf{yes/no interrogative}.

· \textbf{D + C (``fiction books'')} makes a natural noun phrase;
\textbf{``reading fiction books''} (gerund phrase) functions as the
\textbf{subject}.

· \textbf{A (``very interesting'')} is the \textbf{subject complement}
(predicate adjective) after \textbf{be}.

· Structure: \textbf{Be (aux) + Subject (gerund phrase) + Complement
(AdjP)} → \emph{Is {[}reading fiction books{]} {[}very interesting{]}?}
(Add ``?'' to mark interrogation.) \textbf{Why other options are
incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(b) BADC → ``is reading very interesting fiction books''}:
fragment; lacks an explicit subject before the verb \textbf{is}.

· \textbf{(c) BCAD → ``is reading books very interesting fiction''}:
jumbling; treats \textbf{fiction} as predicate noun and misplaces
\textbf{very interesting}.

· \textbf{(d) BACD → ``is reading very interesting books fiction''}:
ill-formed predicate; ``books fiction'' is not a valid complement.

\textbf{Info booster (useful rules):}\\
· \textbf{Yes/No questions with \emph{be}}: \textbf{Be + Subject +
Complement} (e.g., \emph{Is swimming difficult?}).

· \textbf{Gerund phrase as subject}: \emph{Reading fiction books} can
act as a singular subject.

· \textbf{Noun phrase order}: adjective-like noun ( \textbf{fiction}) +
head noun ( \textbf{books}) → \emph{fiction books}.

Q.167 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence. The teacher explained him the topic.}

A. taught him the topic\\
B. told him the topic\\
C. explained the topic to him\\
D. explained to him the topic

Answer: C

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (c)
\textbf{explained the topic to him}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The verb \textbf{explain} does \textbf{not} take a
double object ( \emph{explain someone something} is wrong). The correct
pattern is \textbf{explain + object + to + person} or \textbf{explain to
+ person + object}. Hence,``explained \textbf{the topicto him}'' is the
most natural and standard correction.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· Verbs like \textbf{explain, describe, suggest} require a
\textbf{preposition ``to''} before the indirect object: \textbf{explain
something to someone}.

· Contrast with verbs that take double objects (
\textbf{give/tell/teach/show}): \emph{give him the book}, \emph{tell her
the truth} (no preposition needed).

\textbf{Example:}\\
· Correct: \emph{The guide explained the route to us.}

· Incorrect: \emph{The guide explained us the route.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· Acceptable variant order: \textbf{explain to + person + object}
(option (d) \emph{explained to him the topic} is grammatically possible
but is \textbf{less idiomatic} in standard exam style; (c) is
preferred).· With \textbf{tell}, a double object is fine ( \emph{told
him the topic}), but \textbf{meaning differs} from ``explain'' (tell =
inform; explain = make clear with reasons).

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· (a) \textbf{taught him the topic} --- changes the verb's meaning
(teach ≠ explain).

· (b) \textbf{told him the topic} --- grammatical, but \textbf{tell} ≠
\textbf{explain} (loss of nuance).

· (d) \textbf{explained to him the topic} --- possible, but (c) is the
\textbf{standard, more idiomatic} correction in exams.

Q.168 \textbf{Identify the word in this sentence that contains an
error.}\\
\textbf{She arrived to the office early this morning.}

A. to\\
B. morning\\
C. arrived\\
D. office

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) contains an error.

\textbf{Detailed explanation of error:} The verb \textbf{arrive} takes
the preposition \textbf{at} for specific/smaller places and \textbf{in}
for larger areas (cities/countries). We \textbf{do not} use ``arrive
\textbf{to}'' in standard usage for locations. The sentence should read:
\textbf{``She arrived at the office early this morning.''}\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{arrive at} + point/small place: \emph{arrive at the
office/station/door}.

· \textbf{arrive in} + large area: \emph{arrive in Delhi/India/Europe}.

· \textbf{reach} takes \textbf{no preposition}: \emph{She reached the
office early.}

· \textbf{Example:}\emph{They arrived at the airport on time.} /
\emph{She arrived in Mumbai last night.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· With \textbf{abstract nouns}, use \textbf{arrive at}: \emph{arrive at
a decision/conclusion}.

· ``Arrive to'' may appear in older/literary registers with infinitives
( \emph{arrived to find\ldots{}}), but \textbf{not} for location marking
in contemporary standard English.

Q.169 \textbf{Choose the correct one-word substitution for:}\\
\textbf{"Property inherited from one\textquotesingle s father"}

A. Matrimony\\
B. Patrimony\\
C. Autocracy\\
D. Testimony

Answer: B

Sol: The correct one-word for the given group of words is (b)
\textbf{Patrimony}.

Patrimony means property or estate inherited from one's father or male
ancestors; by extension, any heritage handed down. (Hindi: पैतृकसंपत्ति
\textbf{/} पितृसंपत्ति). \textbf{Example:}\emph{After his father's demise,
Arjun managed the \textbf{patrimony}with great care.}

\textbf{Meanings of the given other options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Matrimony:} marriage; the state of being married. (Hindi:
विवाह).

· \textbf{(c) Autocracy:} government by a single person with absolute
power. (Hindi: एकतंत्र \textbf{/} तानाशाहीशासन).

· \textbf{(d) Testimony:} a formal statement of evidence; witness
statement. (Hindi: गवाही \textbf{/} साक्ष्य).
\end{quote}

