Q.170 \textbf{Choose the correct meaning of Idiom "To oil the hinges of
silence".}

A. To bribe someone to remain quiet\\
B. To prevent secrets from being exposed\\
C. To gradually break uncomfortable silence\\
D. To speak diplomatically in tense moments

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) is the correct meaning of the given idiom.

Given Idiom: \emph{To oil the hinges of silence} --- Meaning: to keep
someone silent by giving money or favour; to ``grease'' the situation so
that no sound (disclosure) occurs. \emph{(Hindi:}
किसीकोचुपरहनेकेलिएरिश्वत\emph{/}अनुग्रहदेना\emph{;} मुँहबन्दीकराना\emph{).}

Example: The whistleblower was offered hush money to oil the hinges of
silence before the report was published.

Other related idioms and their meanings:\\
· Grease someone's palm: to bribe someone.
\emph{(}हाथगर्मकरना\emph{/}रिश्वतदेना\emph{)}\\
· Hush money: money paid to keep someone from revealing information.
\emph{(}मुँहबन्दीकीरकम\emph{)}\\
· Keep under wraps: to keep something secret. \emph{(}गोपनीयरखना\emph{)}

Q.171 \textbf{Find the incorrect part of the sentence:}\\
\textbf{She is more smarter than her sister.}

A. No error\\
B. more smarter\\
C. She is\\
D. than her sister

Answer: B

Sol: Option (b) contains an error.

\textbf{Explanation:} This is a \textbf{double comparative}. With short
adjectives like \emph{smart}, we form the comparative by adding
\textbf{--er} ( \emph{smarter}), \textbf{not} by using \emph{more} +
--er. Hence, the correct sentence is: \textbf{She is smarter than her
sister.}

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:} Use \textbf{--er / --est} with most
one-syllable (and some two-syllable) adjectives ( \emph{fast → faster}).
Use \textbf{more/most} with longer adjectives ( \emph{more
intelligent}). \textbf{Do not combine}\emph{more} with the\textbf{--er}
form ( \emph{more smarter}, \emph{most fastest} are incorrect).

\textbf{Example:} Incorrect: \emph{This road is more narrower.}✔
Correct: \emph{This road is narrower.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· Some two-syllable adjectives can take either form (e.g.,
\emph{cleverer} / \emph{more clever}; exam preference often favors one
form consistently---stick to \textbf{one}, not both).

· With adjectives that \textbf{do not} take --er (e.g.,
\emph{intelligent}, \emph{beautiful}), use \textbf{more} ( \emph{more
intelligent}, \emph{more beautiful}).

Q.172 \textbf{Which of the given options is the most logical sequencing
of the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph?}

\textbf{1. Feminism began as a fight for women\textquotesingle s
suffrage and then expanded to include social discrimination, workplace
inequalities and reproductive rights. 2. Nevertheless, the movement
received significant support from progressive thinkers who believed in
gender equality and the dismantling of patriarchal structures.}

\textbf{3. Conservatives viewed the feminist movement as threatening to
family values, with fears that women would abandon traditional roles as
wives and mothers. 4. The evolution of feminism has been a
transformative journey, reflecting changing societal attitudes and the
struggles for gender equality.}

A. 3-4-2-1\\
B. 1-3-4-2\\
C. 2-3-1-4\\
D. 4-1-3-2

Answer: D

Sol: Option (d) is the correct order of the given sentences.

· \textbf{4} is the topic sentence introducing the \textbf{evolution of
feminism} in general terms---broad, thematic opener.

· \textbf{1} naturally follows as it \textbf{chronologically} sketches
the movement's early aims (suffrage) and its later
expansion---supporting detail for the ``evolution.''· \textbf{3}
introduces \textbf{opposition} (conservatives' fears), providing a
balanced discourse after the developmental account in (1).

· \textbf{2} begins with the contrast marker \textbf{``Nevertheless,''}
which \textbf{resolves} (3) by presenting counter-support from
progressives; cohesion via concessive linkage makes \textbf{3 → 2} the
best pair.· Thus, \textbf{4-1-3-2} yields a coherent progression:
overview → history/expansion → resistance → countervailing support.

Q.173 \textbf{Select the one-word term for:}\\
\textbf{"A brief note or explanation added alongside a diagram or
passage to provide clarity or context."}

A. Annotation\\
B. Footnote\\
C. Glossary\\
D. Caption

Answer: A

Sol: The correct one-word for the given group of words is (a)
\textbf{Annotation}.

\textbf{Explanation (}हिंदीअर्थ\textbf{):}\emph{Annotation} means a brief
explanatory note or comment added to a text, image, or diagram to
clarify meaning or provide context (टिप्पणी/व्याख्यात्मकटिप्पणी). It
highlights or explains specific parts for better understanding.

\textbf{Example:} The professor added \textbf{annotations} to the
manuscript to clarify complex theories.

\textbf{Meanings of the given other options:}\\
· \textbf{(b) Footnote}: A note placed at the bottom of a page giving
additional information or citation (पाद-टिप्पणी).

· \textbf{(c) Glossary}: An alphabetical list of terms with definitions,
usually at a book's end (शब्द-सूची).

· \textbf{(d) Caption}: A brief title or explanation under a picture or
illustration (शीर्षक/चित्र-विवरण).

Q.174 \textbf{Select the correct synonym of Modify.}

A. Create\\
B. Alter\\
C. Hide\\
D. Destroy

Answer: B

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (b) \textbf{Alter}.

\textbf{Given word:Modify} --- to change something partially or
slightly, often to improve it or make it more suitable; to make
adjustments without completely transforming its nature. (Hindi:
परिवर्तन\textbf{/}संशोधनकरना.)\\
\textbf{Example (given word):} The editor asked the author to
\textbf{modify} a few paragraphs for clarity.

\textbf{Correct answer word:Alter} --- to change or make different in
character or detail; to adjust or revise something from its original
form. (Hindi: बदलना\textbf{/}परिवर्तितकरना.) \textbf{Example (correct
answer):} You may need to \textbf{alter} the dress so that it fits
better.

\textbf{Synonyms:} change, \textbf{alter}, adjust, amend.

\textbf{Antonyms:} preserve, maintain, retain, keep.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}
