Q.175 \textbf{Rearrange the following steps of the Union Budget
preparation in the correct order:}\\
\textbf{P . The finance minister presents the budget in Parliament}\\
\textbf{Q. The Ministry of Finance collects estimates from various
ministries}\\
\textbf{R. The budget is passed by both houses of Parliament}\\
\textbf{S . The Cabinet approves the final draft of the budget}

A. P→Q→S→R\\
B. Q→S→P→R\\
C. Q→P→R→S\\
D. S→Q→P→R

Answer: B

Sol: Option (b) is the correct order of the given sentences.

· Q → First, the Ministry of Finance collects estimates from various
ministries; without inputs/receipts-expenditure statements, drafting
cannot start.· S → These inputs are consolidated and the Cabinet
approves the final draft before any public presentation.

· P → The Finance Minister presents the Budget in Parliament
(traditionally in the Lok Sabha).

· R → Finally, after debate and voting, the Budget is passed by both
Houses of Parliament.

This sequence reflects a logical administrative (collection → approval →
presentation → passage) flow.

Q.176 \textbf{Identify the error in the sentence.}\\
\textbf{He has saw the movie three times already.}

A. movie\\
B. three\\
C. already\\
D. saw

Answer: D

Sol: Option (d) contains an error.

\textbf{Detailed explanation of error:} In the present perfect tense we
use \textbf{has/have + past participle (V3)}. The verb after ``has''
must be \textbf{seen} (V3), not \textbf{saw} (V2). Therefore, the
correct sentence is: \textbf{``He has seen the movie three times
already.''} ``Already'' is commonly used with the present perfect to
show that the action happened sooner than expected.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{Present Perfect = has/have + V3 (past participle)} to express
an action that has occurred at an indefinite time before now or
continues up to the present.

· Time expressions often used: \emph{already, yet, ever, never, just,
recently, lately, so far}.

\textbf{Example:}\\
· Incorrect: \emph{She has ate breakfast.}

· Correct: \emph{She has \textbf{eaten}breakfast.}

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· Do \textbf{not} combine \emph{has/have} with V2 forms ( \emph{went,
saw, did}). Always use V3 ( \emph{gone, seen, done}).

· With \textbf{be} in perfect tenses: \emph{has been}, not \emph{has
was}.

Q.177 \textbf{Find the incorrect part of the sentence:}\\
\textbf{Neither Ram nor his brothers is reliable.}

A. Neither Ram\\
B. is reliable\\
C. No error\\
D. nor his brothers

Answer: B

Sol: \textbf{Option (b) contains an error.}

\textbf{Detailed explanation of error:} With \textbf{`neither \ldots{}
nor'}, the \textbf{verb agrees with the subject nearest to it}
(proximity rule). Here, the nearer subject is \textbf{``his brothers''}
(plural). Therefore, the verb must be \textbf{plural}:\emph{``are
reliable,''} not \emph{``is reliable.''}\\
\textbf{Corrected sentence:}\emph{Neither Ram nor his brothers
\textbf{are}reliable.}

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· When subjects are joined by \textbf{either\ldots or /
neither\ldots nor}, the verb agrees in \textbf{number and person with
the subject closest to it}.

· If one subject is singular and the other plural, place the plural
subject near the verb to ensure a plural verb.

\textbf{Example:}\\
· \emph{Neither the teacher nor the students \textbf{were}present.}

· \emph{Neither the students nor the teacher \textbf{was}present.}

\textbf{Information booster / exceptions:}\\
· With \textbf{mixed persons} (1st, 2nd, 3rd), the verb agrees with the
nearest subject, but pronoun order in coordinated subjects often follows
courtesy order (2nd--3rd--1st) in formal style; agreement still follows
proximity.

· If the coordinated subjects form a \textbf{single idea} or refer to
the \textbf{same person/thing}, a singular verb may be used (not
applicable here).

Q.178 \textbf{Choose the correct indirect speech form of the following
sentence.}

\textbf{The labourers said to the contractor, "Did you ask the manager
when we are going to receive our remuneration that has been pending for
the last three months?"}

A. The labourers asked the contractor if he asked the manager when they
were going to receive their remuneration that had been pending for the
previous three months.

B. The labourers asked the contractor if he had asked the manager when
they are going to receive their remuneration that was pending for the
previous three months.

C. The labourers asked the contractor if had he asked the manager when
they are going to receive their remuneration that was pending for the
last three months. D. The labourers asked the contractor if he had asked
the manager when they were going to receive their remuneration that had
been pending for the previous three months.

Answer: D

Sol: The correct indirect speech is (d).

\textbf{Rules of conversion (applied here):}\\
1. \textbf{Reporting verb ``said to'' → ``asked''} (interrogative).

2. \textbf{Yes/No question} → introduce \textbf{if/whether}; maintain
embedded \textbf{wh-clause} (``when \ldots'').

3. \textbf{Backshift of tenses} because the reporting verb is past (
\textbf{said}):\\
· \textbf{Did you ask} → \textbf{if he had asked} (past perfect).

· \textbf{we are going to receive} → \textbf{they were going to receive}
(present progressive of ``be going to'' backshifts to past
progressive).\\
· \textbf{has been pending} → \textbf{had been pending} (present perfect
→ past perfect).

4. \textbf{Pronoun changes}: \textbf{you → he}, \textbf{we → they},
\textbf{our → their}.

5. \textbf{Time expression}: \textbf{the last three months → the
previous three months}.

\textbf{Why the other options are wrong:}\\
· (a) uses \textbf{if he asked} (simple past) instead of \textbf{had
asked} (past perfect) after ``Did you ask\ldots?'' and keeps backshift
inconsistent.\\
· (b) keeps \textbf{they are going} (no backshift) and uses \textbf{was
pending} (should be \textbf{had been pending}).

· (c) misorders \textbf{if had he asked} (incorrect interrogative word
order in indirect speech) and lacks proper backshift elsewhere.
\end{quote}

