Q.179 \textbf{Convert the sentence provided below from its passive voice
structure to an active voice structure:}\\
\textbf{It is believed that the minister will announce the new reforms
next week.}

A. People think the reforms have already been announced.

B. People believe that the minister will announce the new reforms next
week.\\
C. The new reforms are announced by the minister.

D. It was believed that the reforms would be announced.

Answer: B

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is (b).

\textbf{Explanation}: The original sentence uses an \textbf{impersonal
passive} construction: \emph{``It is believed that \ldots''} To convert
to active, we introduce a general/indefinite subject such as
\textbf{People} (or``They/Experts/Observers'') and keep the that-clause
intact: \textbf{People believe that the minister will announce the new
reforms next week.} This preserves tense (present simple passive
\textbf{is believed} →present simple active \textbf{believe}) and
meaning.

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Active voice:} Subject (People) + \textbf{believe} + that +
{[}subject + will + base verb{]}.

· \textbf{Passive voice (impersonal):It + is + past participle
(believed) + that + {[}clause{]}}.

\textbf{Rules of conversion (Passive → Active) with ``It is
said/believed/thought that \ldots''}\\
1. Replace the dummy subject \textbf{It} with a generic subject (
\textbf{People/They/Experts}).

2. Change the passive reporting verb ( \textbf{is
believed/said/thought}) to its active counterpart (
\textbf{believe/say/think}) in the correct tense.

3. Retain the \textbf{that-clause} unchanged in content and future form
( \textbf{will announce} stays \textbf{will announce}).

\textbf{Example:}\\
· Passive: \emph{It is said that the show will be cancelled.}

· Active: \emph{People say that the show will be cancelled.}

\textbf{Information booster:} In formal writing, the impersonal passive
(``It is believed/said\ldots'') is often preferred to avoid specifying
the agent; in tests, converting to active typically calls for a generic
plural subject like \textbf{People}.

Q.180 \textbf{Choose the correct meaning of Idiom "Bite the bullet".}

A. Endure something painful\\
B. Make a bold plan\\
C. Speak without thinking\\
D. Tell a secret

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) is the correct meaning of the given idiom.

\textbf{Given Idiom: ``Bite the bullet''}\\
\textbf{Meaning:} To \textbf{force oneself to face or endure} a painful,
difficult, or unpleasant situation with courage and resolve; to accept
hardship and carry on. \emph{(Hindi:} दाँतभींचकरसहना \emph{/}
कड़वीगोलीनिगलना\emph{)} \textbf{Example:}\emph{I hate going to the
dentist, but I'll \textbf{bite the bullet}and book the appointment.}

\textbf{Other related idioms and their meanings:}\\
· \textbf{Grin and bear it:}सहतेहुएमुस्कुराना---endure something unpleasant
without complaining.

· \textbf{Face the music:}परिणामों/आलोचनाकासामनाकरना---accept the
unpleasant consequences.

· \textbf{Take it on the chin:}हिम्मतसेसहना---endure criticism or hardship
bravely.

· \textbf{Swallow the bitter pill:}कड़वासच/कठिनाईकोस्वीकारकरना---accept an
unpleasant fact or situation.

Q.181 \textbf{What does the idiom \textquotesingle Not
one\textquotesingle s cup of tea\textquotesingle{} mean?}

A. Impossible task\\
B. Very easy\\
C. Something disliked\\
D. Something enjoyable

