Q.182 \textbf{Which of the given options is the most logical sequencing
of the following sentences to form a meaningful paragraph?}\\
\textbf{1. It cut off oxygen and prevented the usual decay caused by
air, moisture, and bacteria.}

\textbf{2. The excavation of Pompeii provided a remarkably
well-preserved glimpse into life in ancient Rome.}

\textbf{3. When the eruption occurred, a thick layer of hot ash and
pumice rapidly buried the town.}

\textbf{4. Unlike other ruins, Pompeii remained frozen in time due to
Mount Vesuvius\textquotesingle{} sudden eruption.}

A. 1-2-4-3\\
B. 3-1-4-2\\
C. 4-2-3-1\\
D. 2-4-3-1

Answer: D

Sol: \textbf{Option (d)} is the correct order of the given sentences.

· \textbf{(2)} introduces the topic with the result of
\textbf{excavation}---a neutral, broad opening: a well-preserved glimpse
into Roman life.

· \textbf{(4)} explains \emph{why} preservation was exceptional: Pompeii
remained ``frozen in time'' due to the sudden eruption---linking the
result to its cause.

· \textbf{(3)} provides the concrete \textbf{event detail} of the
eruption: rapid burial by ash and pumice.

· \textbf{(1)} gives the \textbf{mechanism}: the ash layer cut off
oxygen, preventing decay---this must follow (3) because the pronoun ``
\textbf{It}'' refers to the ash/pumice deposit mentioned in (3).· Thus,
the coherent causal chain is \textbf{Result → Reason → Event →
Mechanism} = \textbf{2-4-3-1}.

Q.183 \textbf{Fill in the blank with appropriate parts of speech.}\\
\textbf{We spent an \_\_\_\_\_ day at school yesterday}

A. enjoyable\\
B. enjoying\\
C. enjoyment\\
D. enjoyed

Answer: A
\end{quote}

Copyright © 2026 Adda247

\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (a) \textbf{enjoyable}.

\textbf{Explanation:} We need an adjective to modify the noun
\textbf{day}. \textbf{Enjoyable} is an adjective meaning ``pleasant;
giving pleasure or satisfaction'' (आनंददायक/मनोरंजक). Hence, ``an
enjoyable day'' is the natural, grammatically correct collocation.

\textbf{Example:} We had an \textbf{enjoyable} picnic by the lake.

\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
· \textbf{(b) enjoying:} Present participle/gerund
(क्रियावाचकसंज्ञा/वर्तमानकृदन्त). ``An enjoying day'' is unidiomatic;
\emph{enjoying} normally describes a person (``enjoying the show''), not
the day.

· \textbf{(c) enjoyment:} Noun (आनंद/आनंद-प्राप्ति). ``An enjoyment day'' is
incorrect; a noun cannot directly premodify \textbf{day} here.

· \textbf{(d) enjoyed:} Past participle (भूतकृदन्त). ``An enjoyed day'' is
unnatural; we might say ``a \textbf{much-enjoyed} day,'' but
\emph{enjoyed} alone is not idiomatic before \textbf{day}.

Q.184 \textbf{Choose the antonym of
\textquotesingle affluent\textquotesingle:}

A. Wealthy\\
B. Rich\\
C. Destitute\\
D. Prosperous

Answer: C

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (c) \textbf{Destitute}.

\textbf{Given word (Affluent)}: Affluent means very wealthy; having an
abundance of money, possessions, or other material goods. It suggests
prosperity and easy access to resources and comforts.
\emph{(Hindi:}समृद्ध\emph{,} धनवान\emph{)}\\
\textbf{Example}: The \textbf{affluent} neighborhood has well-maintained
parks and schools.

\textbf{Correct answer word (Destitute)}: Destitute means lacking the
basic necessities of life---so poor that one is deprived of food,
shelter, or clothing. It conveys extreme poverty and absence of
resources.

\emph{(Hindi:} अत्यंतदरिद्र\emph{,} निराधार\emph{)}\\
\textbf{Example}: After the disaster, many families were left
\textbf{destitute} and in need of urgent assistance.

\textbf{Synonyms (of affluent)}: wealthy, prosperous, well-off, opulent.

\textbf{Antonyms (of affluent)}: destitute, impoverished, penniless,
needy.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Wealthy}: having a great deal of money, resources, or
assets (Hindi: धनी, संपन्न)\\
· \textbf{(b) Rich}: possessing large amounts of money or valuable
possessions; abundant (Hindi: अमीर, समृद्ध)\\
· \textbf{(d) Prosperous}: successful and flourishing, especially
financially (Hindi: समृद्ध, उन्नत)

Q.185 \textbf{What is the place for ammunition and weapons called?}

A. Gallery\\
B. Library\\
C. Arsenal\\
D. Laboratory

Answer: C

Sol: The correct one-word for the given group of words is (c)
\textbf{Arsenal}.

\textbf{Explanation:} An \textbf{arsenal} is a storehouse, depot, or
facility where arms, ammunition, and military equipment are kept and
often manufactured or maintained. (Hindi: शस्त्रागार\textbf{/}हथियारभंडार.)
\textbf{Example:} The city's \textbf{arsenal} supplies weapons and
ammunition to nearby military bases.

\textbf{Meanings of the given other options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Gallery}: a room or building for displaying art or for
spectators. (Hindi: चित्रशाला\textbf{/}दीर्घा)\\
· \textbf{(b) Library}: a place where books and other informational
materials are kept for reading/borrowing. (Hindi: पुस्तकालय)\\
· \textbf{(d) Laboratory}: a room/place equipped for scientific
experiments or research. (Hindi: प्रयोगशाला)

Q.186 \textbf{Select the correct passive form of the given sentence.}\\
\textbf{"The manager will approve the application."}

A. The application has been approved.

B. The application will be approved by the manager.\\
C. The application was being approved.

D. The application is approved by the manager.

Answer: B

Sol: The correct passive voice of the given sentence is (b).

\textbf{Explanation:} The active sentence is in \textbf{Simple Future}
tense: \emph{will + base verb}. To convert to passive, use \textbf{will
+ be + V3} and make the object the new subject.·
\textbf{Active:}\emph{The manager (S) will approve (V) the application
(O).}

· \textbf{Passive:}\emph{The application (S) \textbf{will be
approved}(V3) by the manager (by + agent).}

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Active voice:} Subject + \textbf{will} + V1 + Object\\
· \textbf{Passive voice:} Object (now Subject) + \textbf{will be} + V3
(+ by + Original Subject)\\
\textbf{Examples:}\\
· Active: \emph{They will announce the results tomorrow.}

· Passive: \emph{The results \textbf{will be announced}tomorrow.}

· Active: \emph{We will send the documents next week.}

· Passive: \emph{The documents \textbf{will be sent}next week.}

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· With \textbf{modals} (can, may, must, should, will, shall, might,
could), passive = \textbf{modal + be + V3}.

· The \textbf{``by + agent''} phrase is optional if the agent is unknown
or unnecessary: \emph{The application will be approved (soon).}

Q.187 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to fill in the
blank.}\\
\textbf{The soldiers died \_\_\_ their country.}

A. in\\
B. at\\
C. by\\
D. for

Answer: D

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (d) for.

Explanation: In idiomatic English we say ``die for one's country,''
where for expresses purpose/benefit or in whose cause something is done.
(Hindi: for = \textbf{केलिए}.) Hence, ``The soldiers died for their
country'' conveys sacrifice on behalf of the nation.

Example: Many martyrs have died for their motherland.

Explain why other options are incorrect.

· (a) in: ``in'' (में) denotes location/area (``die in battle,'' ``in
war''), but the sentence needs purpose/benefit, not mere location.

· (b) at: ``at'' (पर) marks a point/place (``at the border''), not
purpose/benefit; ``died at their country'' is wrong.

· (c) by: ``by'' (द्वारा) shows agency/means; we don't say ``died by
their country.'' For cause we use ``of/from'' (died of cancer) or for
purpose ``for.''
\end{quote}

