Q.188 \textbf{Choose the sentence that uses a homonym of the highlighted
word:}\\
\textbf{\emph{He had to} tear \emph{the envelope to open it.}}

A. The comedian caused the audience to \textbf{tear} up with laughter.\\
B. He found a small \textbf{tear} in the corner.

C. Please don\textquotesingle t \textbf{tear} the paper again.

D. She used tape to fix the \textbf{tear} on the bag.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct sentence using a homonym is (a).

\textbf{Explanation:} In the stem, \textbf{tear} (pronounced /teər/)
means \emph{to rip}. In option (a), \textbf{tear up} uses \textbf{tear}
(pronounced /tɪər/) meaning \emph{to begin to cry; produce tears} (हिंदी:
आँसूआजाना). Same spelling, different meaning and pronunciation → a classic
\textbf{homonym/homograph} case.

\textbf{Why the others are not correct:}\\
· \textbf{(b) a small tear} = a \emph{rip} (noun), semantically the same
root meaning as the stem (rip), not a different lexical meaning.

· \textbf{(c) don't tear the paper} = the same verb \emph{to rip} as the
stem.

· \textbf{(d) fix the tear} = noun \emph{rip} again.

· Hence, only (a) genuinely shifts to the ``tears in the eyes'' meaning.

Q.189 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence.}\\
\textbf{\emph{He} denied to help me.}

A. help to me\\
B. for helping me\\
C. helping me\\
D. to helping me

Answer: C

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (c)
\textbf{helping me}.

\textbf{Explanation:} After \textbf{deny}, we use a \textbf{gerund (-ing
form)} or a \textbf{that-clause}, not an infinitive. Therefore, \emph{He
denied helping me} is idiomatic and grammatically correct. The verb
\emph{deny} takes objects like \emph{denying wrongdoing / denying
stealing the money}, not \emph{deny to + V1}.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\emph{deny + gerund} / \emph{deny +
(that)-clause}.

\textbf{Example:}\emph{She denied taking the documents.} / \emph{He
denied that he knew the plan.}

\textbf{Information booster (common verbs followed by gerund):} admit,
avoid, consider, delay, deny, enjoy, finish, keep, mind, miss, postpone,
risk, suggest, recommend, practice, appreciate, etc.

Q.190 \textbf{Choose the correctly spelt word:}

A. Mischievous\\
B. Mischievious\\
C. Mischeivous\\
D. Mishevious

Answer: A

Sol: Option (a) is the \textbf{correctly} spelt word.

\textbf{Correct spelling is:} `` \textbf{Mischievous}'' --- adjective
meaning ``fond of causing or showing playful mischief; teasingly
troublesome; sometimes causing minor harm'' (शरारती/उत्पाती).
\textbf{Example:} The \textbf{mischievous} child hid behind the curtain
and giggled.

\textbf{Parts of speech note (same-word spelling
set):}\emph{Mischievous} is an \textbf{adjective}. The other options are
incorrect spellings of the \textbf{same adjective}.

Q.191 \textbf{Convert the sentence provided below from its passive voice
structure to an active voice structure: The groceries are delivered to
the house by the shopkeeper every evening.}

A. The shopkeeper delivers the groceries to the house every evening.\\
B. The groceries were delivered to the house by the shopkeeper.

C. The shopkeeper had delivered the groceries to the house.\\
D. The groceries are being delivered to the house every evening.

Answer: A

Sol: The correct active/passive voice of the given sentence is (a).

\textbf{Explanation}: The original is in \textbf{simple present
passive}: \emph{are delivered} (be + past participle) with the agent in
a \emph{by}-phrase ( \emph{by the shopkeeper}). To change to
\textbf{active}, make the agent the subject ( \textbf{The shopkeeper}),
keep the tense in simple present ( \textbf{delivers}), and retain the
object and adverbials ( \emph{the groceries \ldots{} every evening}).
Thus: \textbf{``The shopkeeper delivers the groceries to the house every
evening.''}\\
\textbf{Rules of conversion (Passive → Active) with structure in
detail.}

\textbf{Structure:}\\
· \textbf{Passive Voice (simple present):} Subject (receiver) +
\emph{am/is/are} + V³ + (by + agent) + modifiers.

· \textbf{Active Voice (simple present):} Subject (doer) + V¹(s/es) +
Object + modifiers.

\textbf{Active voice:}\emph{The shopkeeper delivers the groceries to the
house every evening.}

\textbf{Passive voice:}\emph{The groceries are delivered to the house by
the shopkeeper every evening.}

\textbf{Example (rule illustration):}\\
· Passive: \emph{Letters are written by Riya every day.}

· Active: \emph{Riya writes letters every day.}

\textbf{Information booster:} In habitual/general truths, keep the
\textbf{same tense} in conversion. Remove the \emph{by}-phrase if the
agent is unknown/irrelevant; otherwise make it the active subject.

Q.192 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence.}\\
\textbf{He said me a lie.}

A. told me a lie\\
B. said to me lie\\
C. told me lie\\
D. said lie

Answer: A

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (a)
\textbf{told me a lie}.

\textbf{\#Explanation:\#} In standard usage, \textbf{tell} takes a
person as its indirect object without \textbf{to} (``tell \textbf{me}
something''), while \textbf{say} generally takes either quoted speech or
a content clause and, if a person is mentioned, it requires \textbf{to}
(``say \textbf{to me}''). Also, the natural collocation is
\textbf{``tell a lie / tell the truth.''} Hence, ``He \textbf{told me a
lie}'' is idiomatic and grammatical.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{say}: say + ``something'' (+ \textbf{to} + person) → \emph{He
said, ``Stop.'' / He said to me that\ldots{}}\\
· \textbf{tell}: tell + person + ``something'' (no \textbf{to} before
the person) → \emph{He told me a story / He told me that\ldots{}}\\
· \textbf{Example:}\emph{She \textbf{told me the truth}, but later she
\textbf{said to me}that she regretted it.}

· \textbf{Information booster:} Fixed phrases include \textbf{tell a lie
/ tell the truth / tell a story / tell the time}. We do \textbf{not} say
\emph{``say me a lie''} or \emph{``told to me a lie.''}
\end{quote}

