Q.193 \textbf{Select the most appropriate homonym.}\\
\textbf{The old fort was} taken bystorm\textbf{.}

A. Assault\\
B. Tornado\\
C. Rain\\
D. Breeze

Answer: A

Sol: The correct homonym of the given word in context is (a) Assault.

Given word (storm): In this sentence, storm is used metaphorically to
mean a sudden, forceful attack (not a weather event). (Hindi:
\textbf{आकस्मिकआक्रमण}/\textbf{धावा}) Example: The army took the city by
storm, overwhelming the defenses.

Correct answer word (assault):Assault means a violent or forceful
attack, often sudden and determined, aimed at seizing control. (Hindi:
\textbf{आक्रमण}, \textbf{आकस्मिकहमला}) Example: The fort fell after a
fierce assault on its main gate.

Synonyms (contextual): attack, onslaught, raid, charge.

Antonyms: defense, protection, guard, repulse.

Meanings of all the other given options:\\
· (b) Tornado: A violent rotating column of air; a whirlwind. (Hindi:
\textbf{बवंडर})\\
· (c) Rain: Water droplets falling from clouds. (Hindi:
\textbf{बारिश})\\
· (d) Breeze: A light gentle wind. (Hindi:
\textbf{समीर}/\textbf{हल्कीहवा})

Q.194 \textbf{Choose the antonym of
\textquotesingle commend\textquotesingle:}

A. Praise\\
B. Approve\\
C. Criticize\\
D. Applaud

Answer: C

Sol: The correct antonym of the given word is (c) \textbf{criticize}.

\textbf{Given word: `commend' --- meaning (}सराहनाकरना\textbf{,}
प्रशंसाकरना\textbf{)}\\
To \textbf{commend} means to praise formally or officially; to speak
favorably of someone's actions, qualities, or work. It often implies
public approval or endorsement.

\textbf{Example:}\emph{The manager \textbf{commended}the team for
meeting the deadline.}

\textbf{Correct answer word: `criticize' --- meaning
(}आलोचनाकरना\textbf{)}\\
To \textbf{criticize} means to find fault with or express disapproval of
something or someone, often by pointing out shortcomings or mistakes. It
stands in direct semantic opposition to praising/commending.
\textbf{Example:}\emph{Reviewers \textbf{criticized}the film for its
weak storyline.}

\textbf{Synonyms (for `commend'):} praise, applaud, laud, extol.

\textbf{Antonyms (of `commend'):} criticize, censure, condemn, denounce.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Praise:} to express warm approval or admiration
(प्रशंसाकरना).

· \textbf{(b) Approve:} to accept or regard as satisfactory; to sanction
(मंज़ूरकरना).

· \textbf{(d) Applaud:} to show approval by clapping or acclaim; to
praise (तालियाँबजाकरसराहनाकरना).

Q.195 \textbf{Select the synonym of Vigilant.}

A. Inattentive\\
B. Sleepy\\
C. Alert\\
D. Careless

Answer: C

Sol: The correct synonym of the given word is (c) \textbf{Alert}.

\textbf{Given word (Vigilant)}: Vigilant means keeping careful watch for
possible danger or difficulties; being attentive and on guard. It
implies sustained, deliberate awareness. \emph{(Hindi:} चौकस\emph{,}
सतर\textbf{Example}: The guards remained \textbf{vigilant} throughout
the night to prevent any breach.

\textbf{Correct answer word (Alert)}: Alert means quick to notice and
respond to potential problems or threats; watchful and attentive. It
often stresses readiness for action. \emph{(Hindi:} सावधान\emph{,}
चौकन्ना\emph{)} \textbf{Example}: Drivers must stay \textbf{alert} on
foggy roads.

\textbf{Synonyms}: watchful, attentive, observant, wary.

\textbf{Antonyms}: negligent, careless, inattentive, oblivious.

\textbf{Meanings of all the other given options:}\\
· \textbf{(a) Inattentive}: not paying attention; negligent (Hindi:
असावधान, अन्यमनस्क)\\
· \textbf{(b) Sleepy}: needing sleep; drowsy (Hindi: नींदसेभरा, उनींदा)\\
· \textbf{(d) Careless}: not giving sufficient attention or thought;
negligent (Hindi: लापरवाह, बेपरवाह)

Q.196 \textbf{Transform the following sentence into active voice.}

\textbf{The royal sword is said to have been transferred to a new museum
where it would have been preserved properly.}

A. Someone is said to transfer the royal sword to a new museum where
they would have preserved it properly.\\
B. Someone is said to have transferred the royal sword to a new museum
which they would have preserved it properly.

C. Someone is said to have transferred the royal sword to a new museum
where they would preserve it properly.\\
D. Someone is said to have transferred the royal sword to a new museum
where they would have preserved it properly.

Answer: D

Sol:\\
\textbf{The correct active voice of the given sentence is (d).}

\textbf{Explanation:}\\
1. In the original, the core action \textbf{``transferred''} is
expressed with a \textbf{perfect passive infinitive}: \emph{to have been
transferred}. To change this into active voice while keeping the
evidential/reporting frame \textbf{``is said''}, we introduce an
\textbf{indefinite subject} (e.g., \emph{someone/they}) and convert the
passive infinitive to a \textbf{perfect active infinitive}: \emph{to
have transferred}. Hence, \textbf{``Someone is said to have transferred
the royal sword \ldots''}\\
2. The relative clause \emph{``where it would have been preserved
properly''} is also passive. Its active equivalent uses an indefinite
subject ( \emph{they} = museum authorities) and the \textbf{conditional
perfect active}: \textbf{``where they would have preserved it
properly.''}\\
3. Option \textbf{(d)} matches both these requirements exactly: it
preserves tense/aspect ( \textbf{perfect} in the reporting infinitive
and \textbf{conditional perfect} in the relative clause) and correctly
shifts both passive segments to active.

4. \textbf{Grammatical rules used (with structure):}\\
5. \textbf{Reporting verbs + passive pattern + infinitive}\\
a. Passive reporting frame: \textbf{Subject + be + past participle
(said/believed/thought) + to + infinitive}\\
i. Passive perfect infinitive (of main verb): \textbf{to have been +
V₃}\\
b. Active reframe with indefinite agent: \textbf{Someone/They + be +
past participle (said/\ldots) + to have + V₃}\\
c. Example pattern: \emph{The painting is believed \textbf{to have been
stolen}}→ \emph{Someone is believed \textbf{to have stolen}the
painting.}

6. \textbf{Passive → Active in subordinate clause (conditional
perfect):}\\
a. Passive: \textbf{it + would have been + V₃ (by them)}\\
b. Active: \textbf{they + would have + V₃ + it}\\
c. Example: \emph{it would have been preserved (by them)} → \emph{they
would have preserved it.}

7. \textbf{Example (parallel conversion):}\\
8. Passive: \emph{The documents are thought \textbf{to have been
leaked}where they \textbf{would have been destroyed}afterward.}

9. Active: \emph{Someone is thought \textbf{to have leaked}the documents
where \textbf{they would have destroyed}them afterward.}
\end{quote}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 0\tabcolsep) * \real{1.0000}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Why other options are incorrect:}\\
\textbf{(a)}\emph{is said to transfer} → Wrong aspect. The source uses
\textbf{perfect} ( \emph{to have been transferred}), indicating a
\textbf{completed} action; simple infinitive \emph{to transfer} loses
completion. Copyright © 2026 Adda247
\end{quote}\strut
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.48889in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image7.png}
\includegraphics[width=2.33889in,height=0.69028in]{vertopal_3d0b0d13ceb344a0aa13e91d868b487a/media/image11.png}

\textbf{ause error} and redundancy: \emph{``which they would have
preserved it properly''} (double object ``which \ldots{} it''). Also,
``which'' is unsuitable for a \textbf{place}; \textbf{wher(c)}
Downgrades the clause from \textbf{conditional perfect} ( \emph{would
have preserved}) to \textbf{simple conditional} ( \emph{would
preserve}), changing the time/aspect relation and not fully mirroring
\emph{``would have been preserved.''}\\
\textbf{Information booster / tips:}\\
When the \textbf{agent is unknown or not important}, use
\textbf{someone/they} to convert a passive infinitive to active while
keeping the reporting frame ( \emph{is said / is believed / is
reported}).

Always \textbf{preserve tense and aspect} in voice transformation:\\
a. \emph{to have been V₃} (perfect passive) ↔ \emph{to have V₃} (perfect
active).

b. \emph{would have been V₃} (conditional perfect passive) ↔ \emph{would
have V₃} (conditional perfect active).

Q.197 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to fill in the
blank.}\\
\textbf{He is afraid \_\_\_\_\_snakes.}

A. to\\
B. of\\
C. with\\
D. for

Answer: B

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (b) of.

Explanation: The standard collocation is ``afraid of + noun/gerund''
(e.g., \emph{afraid of heights/snakes/flying}). The correct sentence is
``He is afraid of snakes.'' ( \emph{Afraid of} = भयभीत/डरताहै).
Example:\emph{Many children are afraid ofthe dark.}

Explain why other options are incorrect:\\
· (a) to: Preposition ``to'' doesn't follow \emph{afraid}; \emph{afraid
to} is used before a verb (e.g., \emph{afraid to speak}), not before a
noun like \emph{snakes}.

· (c) with: \emph{Afraid with} is non-idiomatic in this sense.

· (d) for: \emph{Afraid for} means ``worried about someone's safety,''
e.g., \emph{afraid for her children}, which doesn't fit here.

Information booster:\\
· Common adjective + preposition patterns: \emph{interested in, good at,
dependent on, similar to, keen on, fond of, afraid of, proud of.}

Q.198 \textbf{Choose the most suitable option to replace the highlighted
part of the sentence. If the sentence is already correct, select
\textquotesingle No substitution required\textquotesingle. The research
paper was full of errors because the data is not verified by any
expert.}

A. has not been verified\\
B. is not being verified yet\\
C. No substitution required\\
D. was not verified

Answer: D

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (d)
\textbf{was not verified}.

\textbf{Explanation:} The main clause \textbf{was full of errors} is in
\textbf{past simple}, describing a completed past situation.

The cause in the \textbf{because}-clause should align with that past
timeframe. \textbf{Was not verified} is \textbf{past simple passive} (be
+ V³) and properly indicates that the act of verification did not occur
before/around the time the paper existed with errors. Options (a) and
(b) place the verification in the present/present perfect, creating
tense inconsistency with the clearly past main clause.

\textbf{Grammatical rule used:}\\
· \textbf{Sequence of tenses \& passive voice formation:} When the main
clause is in past simple for a completed event, the reason/cause is
typically expressed in past (often past simple passive: \textbf{was/were
+ past participle}) to maintain temporal consistency.

· \textbf{Passive voice structure:}\emph{be (in past: was/were) + past
participle} → ``was not verified.''\\
\textbf{Example:}\emph{The report failed peer review because the
references \textbf{were not checked}carefully.}

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· \textbf{Data} can be treated as singular (mass noun) in modern usage
--- ``the data \textbf{was} analyzed'' --- especially in general English
and many sciences. Traditional usage may prefer plural --- ``the data
\textbf{were} analyzed.'' Exams often accept the singular in such
contexts unless a plural verb is explicitly tested.

Q.199 \textbf{Select the most appropriate option to improve the bold
part of the sentence. She said that she will go to market later.}

A. shall go\\
B. could go\\
C. can go\\
D. would go

Answer: D

Sol: The correct substitution of the highlighted words is (d)
\textbf{would go}.

\textbf{Explanation:} In \textbf{indirect (reported) speech}, when the
reporting verb is in the \textbf{past} (``she said''), the future modal
\textbf{will} generally backshifts to \textbf{would} to express
\textbf{future-in-the-past}. Therefore, ``She said that she
\textbf{would go} \ldots'' is the correct sequence of tenses. (Note:
``to the market'' is the more idiomatic phrase for place; however, the
tested point here is the modal backshift.)\\
\textbf{Grammatical rule used (Reported Speech Backshift):}\\
· If the reporting verb is \textbf{past} (said, told, etc.), then:\\
· \textbf{will → would}, \textbf{can → could}, \textbf{may → might},
\textbf{shall → should/would} (context-dependent).

· No backshift is needed for universal truths or when the reporting verb
is present.

\textbf{Example:}\emph{He said that he \textbf{would call}me in the
evening.}

\textbf{Information booster:}\\
· Backshift is optional when the reported situation is still true at the
time of reporting; however, competitive exams typically \textbf{require}
backshift after past reporting verbs unless there's a clear reason not
to.

Q.200 \textbf{Choose the correct option to complete the sentence.}\\
\textbf{Do you think it is going to \_\_\_ this afternoon?}

A. reign\\
B. raign\\
C. rain\\
D. rein

Answer: C

Sol: The correct option to fill in the blank is (c) \textbf{rain}.

\textbf{Explanation}: The sentence asks about the weather, so the
noun/verb \textbf{rain} (precipitation) is required. \emph{(Hindi:}
बारिश\emph{/}बरसात\emph{)} It collocates naturally with \emph{``going
to''} for near-future predictions. \textbf{Example}: The sky is cloudy;
it's going to \textbf{rain} soon.

\textbf{Explain why other options are incorrect.}

· \textbf{(a) reign}: the period or act of ruling as a king/queen.
\emph{(}शासन\emph{/}राज\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(b) raign}: incorrect spelling; no such standard English word.
\emph{(}त्रुटिपूर्णवर्तनी\emph{)}\\
· \textbf{(d) rein}: a strap for controlling a horse; figuratively,
control/restraint. \emph{(}लगाम\emph{/}नियंत्रण\emph{)}
\end{quote}

